1
00:01:22,332 --> 00:01:23,667
Doc!

2
00:02:25,228 --> 00:02:27,689
Doc! Doc! Doc!

3
00:02:27,773 --> 00:02:29,524
Doc! Doc!
What?

4
00:02:31,276 --> 00:02:33,612
Okay, relax, Doc.
It's me. It's me! It's Marty.

5
00:02:33,695 --> 00:02:36,156
No, it can't be. I just sent
you back to the future.

6
00:02:36,239 --> 00:02:37,658
Yeah. No, I know.
You did send me

7
00:02:37,741 --> 00:02:39,534
back to the future,
but I'm back.

8
00:02:39,618 --> 00:02:41,787
I'm back
from the future.

9
00:02:44,998 --> 00:02:46,625
Great Scott!

10
00:05:02,803 --> 00:05:04,429
Hey, kids, what time is it?

11
00:05:04,513 --> 00:05:07,057
Howdy Doody time!

12
00:05:08,809 --> 00:05:10,602
Great Scott!

13
00:05:10,685 --> 00:05:14,147
It's Howdy Doody Time

14
00:05:14,231 --> 00:05:19,903
Bob Smith and Howdy Do
Say Howdy Do to you

15
00:05:19,986 --> 00:05:21,738
Howdy Doody time?

16
00:05:26,910 --> 00:05:31,498
Date. Sunday,
November 13, 1955. 7:01 a.m.

17
00:05:31,581 --> 00:05:32,999
Last night's
time travel experiment

18
00:05:33,083 --> 00:05:35,001
was apparently
a complete success.

19
00:05:35,085 --> 00:05:37,671
Lightning struck the clock
tower at precisely 10:04 p.m.

20
00:05:37,754 --> 00:05:40,590
sending the necessary 1.21
gigawatts into the time vehicle,

21
00:05:40,674 --> 00:05:42,884
which vanished in a
brilliant flash of light

22
00:05:42,968 --> 00:05:45,345
leaving a pair of
fire trails behind.

23
00:05:45,428 --> 00:05:47,514
I, therefore, assume that
Marty and the time vehicle

24
00:05:47,597 --> 00:05:51,393
were transported forward
through time into the year 1985.

25
00:05:51,476 --> 00:05:52,561
After that...

26
00:05:52,644 --> 00:05:55,605
After that, I can't
recall what happened.

27
00:05:55,689 --> 00:05:58,483
In fact, I don't even
remember how I got home.

28
00:05:58,567 --> 00:06:00,360
Perhaps
the gigawatt discharge

29
00:06:00,443 --> 00:06:02,163
coupled with the temporal
displacement field

30
00:06:02,237 --> 00:06:03,697
generated by
the time vehicle

31
00:06:03,780 --> 00:06:05,657
caused a disruption
of my own brain waves

32
00:06:05,740 --> 00:06:08,702
resulting in a condition
of momentary amnesia.

33
00:06:08,785 --> 00:06:10,078
Indeed, I now recall
that moments

34
00:06:10,161 --> 00:06:12,080
after the time vehicle
disappeared

35
00:06:12,163 --> 00:06:13,206
into the future,

36
00:06:13,290 --> 00:06:15,375
I saw a vision
of Marty saying

37
00:06:15,458 --> 00:06:17,377
he had come back
from the future.

38
00:06:17,460 --> 00:06:18,460
Hey, Doc!

39
00:06:18,503 --> 00:06:19,897
Undoubtedly, this was
some sort of residual image...

40
00:06:19,921 --> 00:06:20,964
Doc.

41
00:06:26,970 --> 00:06:30,223
Doc, calm down, okay. Just
calm down. It's me. It's Marty.

42
00:06:30,307 --> 00:06:32,851
No, it can't be you! I sent
you back to the future!

43
00:06:32,934 --> 00:06:34,120
That's right, Doc.
That's right,

44
00:06:34,144 --> 00:06:36,122
but I came back again. I
came back from the future.

45
00:06:36,146 --> 00:06:37,206
Don't you
remember last night?

46
00:06:37,230 --> 00:06:38,457
You fainted.
I brought you home.

47
00:06:38,481 --> 00:06:41,067
This can't be happening!
You can't be here!

48
00:06:41,151 --> 00:06:42,420
It doesn't make sense
for you to be here!

49
00:06:42,444 --> 00:06:44,571
I refuse to even believe
that you are here!

50
00:06:44,654 --> 00:06:46,573
Doc, I am here,
and it does make sense.

51
00:06:46,656 --> 00:06:49,200
Look, I came back to
1955 again with you,

52
00:06:49,284 --> 00:06:52,120
the you from 1985, 'cause
we had to get a book from Biff!

53
00:06:52,203 --> 00:06:53,288
So once I got
the book back,

54
00:06:53,371 --> 00:06:55,081
you, that is the you
from 1985,

55
00:06:55,165 --> 00:06:56,976
were in the Delorean, and
it got struck by lightning,

56
00:06:57,000 --> 00:06:59,085
and you got
sent back to 1885!

57
00:06:59,169 --> 00:07:00,503
1885?

58
00:07:02,756 --> 00:07:05,717
It's a very interesting
story, future boy,

59
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:08,511
but there's just one little
thing that doesn't make sense.

60
00:07:08,595 --> 00:07:10,972
If the me of the future
IS now in the past,

61
00:07:11,056 --> 00:07:13,433
how could you
possibly know about it?

62
00:07:13,516 --> 00:07:14,935
You sent me a letter.

63
00:07:15,018 --> 00:07:17,187
"Dear Marty, if my
calculations are correct,

64
00:07:17,270 --> 00:07:18,831
"you will receive this
letter immediately after

65
00:07:18,855 --> 00:07:21,524
"you saw the Delorean
struck by lightning.

66
00:07:21,608 --> 00:07:24,444
"First, let me assure you
that I'm alive and well.

67
00:07:24,527 --> 00:07:28,573
"I've been living happily these
past eight months in the year 1885.

68
00:07:28,657 --> 00:07:30,176
"The lightning bolt
that hit the DeLorean

69
00:07:30,200 --> 00:07:31,451
"caused a gigawatt
overload

70
00:07:31,534 --> 00:07:33,119
"which scrambled
the time circuits,

71
00:07:33,203 --> 00:07:34,788
"activated
the Flux Capacitor,

72
00:07:34,871 --> 00:07:37,791
"and sent me
back to 1885.

73
00:07:37,874 --> 00:07:39,626
"The overload shorted
out the time circuits

74
00:07:39,709 --> 00:07:41,670
"and destroyed
the flying circuits.

75
00:07:41,753 --> 00:07:44,422
"Unfortunately, the car
will never fly again."

76
00:07:44,506 --> 00:07:45,799
It actually flew?

77
00:07:45,882 --> 00:07:47,550
Yeah, we had
a hover conversion done

78
00:07:47,634 --> 00:07:49,135
in the early
21st century.

79
00:07:49,219 --> 00:07:51,137
Incredible!

80
00:07:51,221 --> 00:07:54,307
"I set myself up as a
blacksmith as a front

81
00:07:54,391 --> 00:07:57,686
"while I attempted to repair
the damage to the time circuits.

82
00:07:57,769 --> 00:07:59,604
"Unfortunately,
this proved impossible,

83
00:07:59,688 --> 00:08:01,231
"because suitable
replacement parts

84
00:08:01,314 --> 00:08:04,567
"will not be
invented until 1947.

85
00:08:04,651 --> 00:08:06,194
"However,
I've gotten quite adept

86
00:08:06,277 --> 00:08:09,614
"at shoeing horses
and fixing wagons."

87
00:08:10,740 --> 00:08:13,702
1885! Amazing!

88
00:08:13,785 --> 00:08:17,038
I actually end up as a
blacksmith in the Old West.

89
00:08:17,455 --> 00:08:18,832
Pretty heavy, huh?

90
00:08:18,915 --> 00:08:21,835
"I have buried the DeLorean
in the abandoned Delgado Mine

91
00:08:21,918 --> 00:08:24,254
"adjacent to the old
Boot Hill Cemetery

92
00:08:24,337 --> 00:08:26,506
"as shown on
the enclosed map.

93
00:08:26,589 --> 00:08:28,149
"Hopefully, it should
remain undisturbed

94
00:08:28,216 --> 00:08:31,678
"and preserved until
you uncover it in 1955.

95
00:08:32,929 --> 00:08:36,099
"Inside, you will find
repair instructions.

96
00:08:36,766 --> 00:08:40,228
"My 1955 counterpart..."
That's me.

97
00:08:40,311 --> 00:08:41,831
"...should have no
problem repairing it

98
00:08:41,896 --> 00:08:44,482
"so that you can drive it
back to the future.

99
00:08:44,566 --> 00:08:48,528
"Once you have returned to
1985, destroy the time machine."

100
00:08:48,611 --> 00:08:50,113
Destroy it?

101
00:08:50,196 --> 00:08:53,283
Yeah. Well, it's
a long story, Doc.

102
00:08:53,366 --> 00:08:56,244
"Do not, I repeat,
do not attempt

103
00:08:56,327 --> 00:08:58,371
"to come back here
to get me.

104
00:08:58,955 --> 00:09:03,043
"I am perfectly happy living in
the fresh air and wide-open spaces.

105
00:09:03,126 --> 00:09:05,628
"And I fear that
unnecessary time travel

106
00:09:05,712 --> 00:09:07,380
"only risks
further disruption

107
00:09:07,464 --> 00:09:09,424
"of the space-time
continuum.

108
00:09:09,507 --> 00:09:12,969
"And please take care
of Einstein for me."

109
00:09:14,512 --> 00:09:15,638
Einstein?

110
00:09:15,722 --> 00:09:20,143
He's your dog, Doc. Einstein is
what you call your dog in 1985.

111
00:09:23,146 --> 00:09:25,106
"I know you will
give him a good home.

112
00:09:25,190 --> 00:09:26,733
"Remember to
walk him twice a day

113
00:09:26,816 --> 00:09:29,194
"and that he only
likes canned dog food.

114
00:09:29,277 --> 00:09:32,489
"These are my wishes. Please
respect them and follow them.

115
00:09:32,572 --> 00:09:36,951
"And so, Marty, I now say
farewell and wish you Godspeed.

116
00:09:37,035 --> 00:09:39,579
"You've been a good, kind,
and loyal friend to me,

117
00:09:39,662 --> 00:09:42,373
"and you made a real
difference in my life.

118
00:09:42,457 --> 00:09:44,918
"I will always treasure
our relationship

119
00:09:45,001 --> 00:09:46,920
"and think on you
with fond memories,

120
00:09:47,003 --> 00:09:50,173
"warm feelings, and a
special place in my heart.

121
00:09:51,841 --> 00:09:55,678
"Your friend in time,
Doc Emmett L. Brown.

122
00:09:55,762 --> 00:09:58,389
"September 1st, 1885."

123
00:10:00,391 --> 00:10:02,271
I never knew I could
write anything so touching.

124
00:10:02,352 --> 00:10:04,104
I know. I know, Doc.
It's beautiful.

125
00:10:07,190 --> 00:10:09,067
It's all right,
Copernicus.

126
00:10:09,150 --> 00:10:10,360
Everything is
gonna be fine.

127
00:10:10,443 --> 00:10:13,321
I'm sorry, Doc. It's all my
fault you're stuck back there.

128
00:10:13,404 --> 00:10:14,989
I never should've
let Biff get to me.

129
00:10:15,073 --> 00:10:17,951
There are plenty worse
places to be than the Old West.

130
00:10:18,034 --> 00:10:19,619
I could have ended up
in the Dark Ages.

131
00:10:19,702 --> 00:10:20,971
They probably
would have burned me

132
00:10:20,995 --> 00:10:23,456
at the stake as
a heretic or something.

133
00:10:23,540 --> 00:10:25,125
Let's look at the map.

134
00:10:25,208 --> 00:10:26,936
All right, according
to this, the time vehicle

135
00:10:26,960 --> 00:10:29,963
is sealed off
in a side tunnel.

136
00:10:30,046 --> 00:10:31,548
We may have to blast.

137
00:10:44,727 --> 00:10:45,895
Whoa!

138
00:10:46,771 --> 00:10:48,940
I think you woke up
the dead with that blast.

139
00:10:49,023 --> 00:10:51,734
Take this camera. I want
to document everything!

140
00:10:54,112 --> 00:10:55,572
This reminds me
of the time

141
00:10:55,655 --> 00:10:57,991
I attempted to reach
the center of the Earth.

142
00:10:58,074 --> 00:11:00,618
I'd been reading my
favorite author, Jules Verne.

143
00:11:00,702 --> 00:11:02,495
I spent weeks preparing
that expedition.

144
00:11:02,579 --> 00:11:04,247
I didn't even
get this far.

145
00:11:04,330 --> 00:11:06,875
Of course, I was
only 12 at the time.

146
00:11:06,958 --> 00:11:10,170
You know, it was the
writings of Jules Verne

147
00:11:10,253 --> 00:11:12,755
that had a profound
effect on my life.

148
00:11:12,839 --> 00:11:14,340
It was when I was 11
that I first read

149
00:11:14,424 --> 00:11:16,593
Twenty Thousand
Leagues Under the Sea.

150
00:11:16,676 --> 00:11:18,261
It was then
that I realized

151
00:11:18,344 --> 00:11:21,681
that I must devote
my life to science.

152
00:11:21,764 --> 00:11:24,225
Doc, check it out.
Look at this.

153
00:11:27,979 --> 00:11:29,522
My initials!

154
00:11:29,606 --> 00:11:31,941
Just like in Journey to
the Center of the Earth!

155
00:11:32,025 --> 00:11:33,067
That means
the time machine

156
00:11:33,109 --> 00:11:34,777
must be right through this
wall!

157
00:11:56,299 --> 00:12:01,554
It's been buried in here for
70 years, two months, and 13 days.

158
00:12:01,638 --> 00:12:02,972
Astounding!

159
00:12:03,056 --> 00:12:05,058
"As you see,
the lightning bolt

160
00:12:05,141 --> 00:12:08,186
"shorted out the time
circuit control microchip.

161
00:12:10,355 --> 00:12:12,440
"The attached..."

162
00:12:12,523 --> 00:12:13,566
Schematic.

163
00:12:13,650 --> 00:12:17,737
"Schematic diagram will allow
you to build a replacement unit

164
00:12:17,820 --> 00:12:19,364
"with 1955 components,

165
00:12:19,447 --> 00:12:22,158
"thus restoring the time
machine to perfect working order."

166
00:12:22,242 --> 00:12:24,619
Unbelievable that
this little piece of junk

167
00:12:24,702 --> 00:12:26,704
could be
such a big problem.

168
00:12:29,249 --> 00:12:30,792
No wonder this
circuit failed.

169
00:12:30,875 --> 00:12:32,210
It says,
"Made in Japan."

170
00:12:34,003 --> 00:12:37,507
What do you mean, Doc? All
the best stuff is made in Japan.

171
00:12:39,592 --> 00:12:40,969
Unbelievable!

172
00:12:47,016 --> 00:12:49,435
You know,
when I was a kid,

173
00:12:49,519 --> 00:12:51,646
I always wanted
to be a cowboy.

174
00:12:52,689 --> 00:12:55,233
And now, knowing I'm gonna
spend my future in the past,

175
00:12:55,316 --> 00:12:59,153
it sounds like a wonderful way
to spend my retirement years.

176
00:12:59,237 --> 00:13:02,115
It just occurred to me,
Marty, since I end up in 1885,

177
00:13:02,198 --> 00:13:04,284
perhaps I'm now in
the history books.

178
00:13:04,367 --> 00:13:06,244
I wonder.

179
00:13:06,327 --> 00:13:08,079
Could I go to the library
and look myself up

180
00:13:08,162 --> 00:13:10,415
in the old
newspaper archives?

181
00:13:10,498 --> 00:13:11,624
I don't know, Doc.

182
00:13:11,708 --> 00:13:12,935
You're the one that's
always saying, you know,

183
00:13:12,959 --> 00:13:15,020
it's not good to know too
much about your own destiny.

184
00:13:15,044 --> 00:13:18,548
You're right, Marty.
I know too much already.

185
00:13:18,631 --> 00:13:20,025
Better that I not
attempt to uncover

186
00:13:20,049 --> 00:13:23,803
the circumstances
of my own future.

187
00:13:23,886 --> 00:13:26,848
Copernicus.
Come on, boy!

188
00:13:27,390 --> 00:13:29,684
I'll get him, Doc.
Copernicus!

189
00:13:30,893 --> 00:13:33,730
Copernicus, come on.
Let's go home, boy.

190
00:13:34,647 --> 00:13:36,649
What's wrong?

191
00:13:36,733 --> 00:13:40,028
What's wrong,
Copernicus? Come on.

192
00:13:40,111 --> 00:13:42,780
Come on, let's go home.
Come on. Come on.

193
00:13:46,492 --> 00:13:49,579
Doc! Doc!
Come here!

194
00:13:49,662 --> 00:13:50,913
Quick!

195
00:13:52,749 --> 00:13:56,252
What's wrong, Marty? You
look like you've seen a ghost.

196
00:13:56,336 --> 00:13:58,463
You're not
far off, Doc.

197
00:14:02,717 --> 00:14:04,677
Great Scott!

198
00:14:04,761 --> 00:14:05,887
Check this out.

199
00:14:05,970 --> 00:14:09,140
"Died September 7th,
1885."

200
00:14:09,223 --> 00:14:10,933
That's one week after
you wrote the letter!

201
00:14:11,309 --> 00:14:14,645
"Erected in eternal memory
by his beloved Clara."

202
00:14:15,021 --> 00:14:16,522
Who the hell
is Clara?

203
00:14:16,606 --> 00:14:18,399
Marty, please
don't stand there!

204
00:14:18,483 --> 00:14:20,026
Oh! Right. Sorry.

205
00:14:20,109 --> 00:14:22,070
I gotta get
another picture.

206
00:14:23,446 --> 00:14:27,533
"Shot in the back by Buford
Tannen over a matter of $80"?

207
00:14:28,117 --> 00:14:30,328
What kind of a future
do you call that?

208
00:14:31,412 --> 00:14:33,873
"Buford Tannen
was a notorious gunman

209
00:14:33,956 --> 00:14:36,709
"whose short temper
and a tendency to drool

210
00:14:36,793 --> 00:14:38,711
"earned him
the nickname Mad Dog.

211
00:14:38,795 --> 00:14:40,046
"He was quick
on the trigger

212
00:14:40,129 --> 00:14:42,340
"and bragged that
he had killed 12 men

213
00:14:42,423 --> 00:14:44,217
"not including
Indians or Chinamen."

214
00:14:44,300 --> 00:14:46,010
Does it mention me?
Am I one of the 12?

215
00:14:46,094 --> 00:14:47,136
Just a minute.

216
00:14:47,220 --> 00:14:49,013
"However this claim
cannot be substantiated,

217
00:14:49,097 --> 00:14:50,807
"since precise records
were not kept

218
00:14:50,890 --> 00:14:52,558
"after Tannen shot
a newspaper editor

219
00:14:52,642 --> 00:14:55,478
"who printed an unfavorable
story about him in 1884."

220
00:14:55,561 --> 00:14:56,622
That's why
we can't find anything.

221
00:14:56,646 --> 00:14:57,647
Look.

222
00:14:59,232 --> 00:15:02,110
William McFly and family.
Your relatives?

223
00:15:02,193 --> 00:15:04,737
My great-grandfather's
name was William.

224
00:15:04,821 --> 00:15:07,323
That's him.
Good-looking guy.

225
00:15:09,409 --> 00:15:11,744
McFlys, but no Browns.

226
00:15:11,828 --> 00:15:12,888
Well, maybe it was
a mistake, Doc.

227
00:15:12,912 --> 00:15:14,056
Maybe that grave
wasn't yours.

228
00:15:14,080 --> 00:15:16,374
There could've been another
Emmett Brown back in 1885.

229
00:15:16,457 --> 00:15:17,458
I don't know.
No.

230
00:15:17,542 --> 00:15:18,685
Did you have any
relatives here back then?

231
00:15:18,709 --> 00:15:20,753
The Browns didn't come
to Hill Valley until 1908.

232
00:15:20,837 --> 00:15:22,422
And then they
were the von Brauns.

233
00:15:22,505 --> 00:15:25,049
My father changed our name
during the First World War.

234
00:15:25,133 --> 00:15:26,259
Doc, look.

235
00:15:28,553 --> 00:15:31,556
Great Scott, it's me!

236
00:15:31,639 --> 00:15:34,559
Then it is true,
all of it.

237
00:15:34,642 --> 00:15:38,062
It is me who goes back
there and gets shot.

238
00:15:38,146 --> 00:15:40,565
It's not gonna
happen, Doc.

239
00:15:40,648 --> 00:15:42,233
After you fix
the time circuits

240
00:15:42,316 --> 00:15:43,818
and put new tires
on the Delorean,

241
00:15:43,901 --> 00:15:47,363
I'm going back to 1885,
and I'm bringing you home.

242
00:15:58,916 --> 00:16:00,793
The clothes fit?

243
00:16:00,877 --> 00:16:04,380
Yeah, everything except
the boots, Doc. They're kind of tight.

244
00:16:04,464 --> 00:16:06,799
I don't know. Are you
sure this stuff is authentic?

245
00:16:06,883 --> 00:16:10,219
Of course. Haven't
you ever seen a Western?

246
00:16:11,053 --> 00:16:13,181
Yeah, I have, Doc.

247
00:16:13,264 --> 00:16:16,142
Clint Eastwood never wore
anything like this.

248
00:16:17,393 --> 00:16:19,020
Clint who?

249
00:16:19,103 --> 00:16:21,105
That's right. You
haven't heard of him yet.

250
00:16:21,189 --> 00:16:23,024
Marty, you have
to wear the boots.

251
00:16:23,107 --> 00:16:25,818
You can't wear those
futuristic things back in 1885.

252
00:16:25,902 --> 00:16:27,782
You shouldn't even be
wearing them here in 1955.

253
00:16:27,820 --> 00:16:29,089
All right, Doc. Look,
as soon as I get there,

254
00:16:29,113 --> 00:16:30,323
I'll put them on.
I promise.

255
00:16:30,406 --> 00:16:31,991
Okay. I think
we're about ready.

256
00:16:32,074 --> 00:16:33,409
I put gas in the tank.

257
00:16:33,493 --> 00:16:34,994
Your future clothes
are packed.

258
00:16:35,077 --> 00:16:38,789
Just in case, fresh batteries
for your walkie-talkies.

259
00:16:39,248 --> 00:16:41,751
Oh! What about that
floating device?

260
00:16:41,834 --> 00:16:43,794
Hoverboard.
All right.

261
00:16:45,922 --> 00:16:47,608
You know, Doc, it's gonna
be a hell of a long walk

262
00:16:47,632 --> 00:16:49,342
back to Hill Valley
from here.

263
00:16:49,425 --> 00:16:50,718
It's still
the safest plan.

264
00:16:50,801 --> 00:16:53,513
After all, we can't risk sending
you back into a populated area

265
00:16:53,596 --> 00:16:55,848
or to a spot that's
geographically unknown.

266
00:16:55,932 --> 00:16:57,517
You don't want to
crash into some tree

267
00:16:57,600 --> 00:16:59,310
that once existed
in the past.

268
00:16:59,393 --> 00:17:01,854
This was all
completely open country,

269
00:17:01,938 --> 00:17:04,565
so you'll have plenty of run
out space when you arrive.

270
00:17:04,649 --> 00:17:07,777
Remember, where you're
going, there are no roads.

271
00:17:07,860 --> 00:17:09,060
There's a small cave
over there

272
00:17:09,111 --> 00:17:11,489
which will be a perfect
place to hide the time vehicle.

273
00:17:11,572 --> 00:17:15,326
Well, the new time circuit
control tubes are warmed up.

274
00:17:18,287 --> 00:17:20,081
Time circuits on.

275
00:17:20,164 --> 00:17:21,391
I wrote the letter
on September 1st,

276
00:17:21,415 --> 00:17:23,042
so we'll send you back
the very next day,

277
00:17:23,125 --> 00:17:24,186
September 2nd.
That's a Wednesday.

278
00:17:24,210 --> 00:17:26,879
September 2nd,
1885, 8:00 a.m.

279
00:17:26,963 --> 00:17:28,214
I get shot
on Monday the 7th,

280
00:17:28,297 --> 00:17:29,657
so you have five days
to locate me.

281
00:17:29,715 --> 00:17:31,026
According to my letter,
I'm a blacksmith,

282
00:17:31,050 --> 00:17:33,052
so I probably have
a shop somewhere.

283
00:17:33,719 --> 00:17:35,721
All you have to do is
drive the time vehicle

284
00:17:35,805 --> 00:17:37,139
directly toward
that screen,

285
00:17:37,223 --> 00:17:39,350
accelerating to
88 miles an hour.

286
00:17:39,433 --> 00:17:42,436
Wait a minute. Doc, if I drive
straight towards the screen,

287
00:17:42,520 --> 00:17:44,063
I'm gonna crash
into those Indians.

288
00:17:44,146 --> 00:17:47,483
Marty, you're not thinking
fourth-dimensionally.

289
00:17:47,567 --> 00:17:49,735
You'll instantly be
transported to 1885,

290
00:17:49,819 --> 00:17:51,946
and those Indians
won't even be there.

291
00:17:53,155 --> 00:17:55,032
Right.
Well.

292
00:17:56,742 --> 00:17:59,412
Good luck for
both of our sakes.

293
00:17:59,495 --> 00:18:00,746
See you in the future.

294
00:18:01,914 --> 00:18:02,915
You mean the past.

295
00:18:02,999 --> 00:18:04,000
Exactly.

296
00:18:10,840 --> 00:18:12,842
Happy trails, Marty!

297
00:18:18,431 --> 00:18:19,849
Ready, Marty?

298
00:18:20,850 --> 00:18:22,018
Ready!

299
00:18:22,685 --> 00:18:23,894
Set!

300
00:18:23,978 --> 00:18:25,187
Hi-ho, Silver.

301
00:19:02,558 --> 00:19:04,268
Indians!

302
00:19:43,182 --> 00:19:44,392
The cave!

303
00:20:19,552 --> 00:20:20,845
Charge!

304
00:20:25,015 --> 00:20:26,058
Shit! The cavalry!

305
00:21:17,276 --> 00:21:19,528
Damn, I ripped
the fuel line.

306
00:22:04,365 --> 00:22:08,452
Maggie! Fetch
some water! We got a hurt man here.

307
00:22:16,502 --> 00:22:19,088
Mom! Mom, is that you?

308
00:22:19,171 --> 00:22:20,339
There, there now.

309
00:22:20,422 --> 00:22:23,759
You've been asleep for
nearly six hours now.

310
00:22:23,843 --> 00:22:26,136
I had this
horrible nightmare.

311
00:22:27,847 --> 00:22:31,183
I dreamed... I dreamed
I was in a Western.

312
00:22:32,852 --> 00:22:39,233
I was being chased by all
these Indians and a bear.

313
00:22:39,316 --> 00:22:40,776
Well, you're safe
and sound

314
00:22:40,860 --> 00:22:42,611
here now
at the McFly farm.

315
00:22:43,529 --> 00:22:45,197
McFly farm?

316
00:22:48,409 --> 00:22:51,912
Well, you're
my... You're my, my...

317
00:22:53,664 --> 00:22:54,790
Who are you?

318
00:22:54,874 --> 00:22:57,501
The name is McFly.
Maggie McFly.

319
00:22:57,585 --> 00:23:01,130
McFly. Maggie?

320
00:23:01,505 --> 00:23:04,675
And that's Mrs. McFly, and
don't you be forgetting the Mrs.

321
00:23:04,758 --> 00:23:06,051
And what might
your name be, sir?

322
00:23:06,135 --> 00:23:07,970
Well, it's...

323
00:23:11,515 --> 00:23:12,850
Eastwood.

324
00:23:14,643 --> 00:23:16,812
Clint Eastwood.

325
00:23:17,271 --> 00:23:19,064
Well, you hit your head,
Mr. Eastwood.

326
00:23:19,148 --> 00:23:20,774
Not too serious,
but lucky for you,

327
00:23:20,858 --> 00:23:22,902
Seamus found you
when he did.

328
00:23:22,985 --> 00:23:24,737
Seamus.

329
00:23:24,820 --> 00:23:26,280
Me husband.

330
00:23:27,990 --> 00:23:32,161
You'll be excusing me,
Mr. Eastwood, while I tend to William.

331
00:23:32,244 --> 00:23:33,245
William.

332
00:23:33,495 --> 00:23:36,123
Oh, that's okay, Will. It's okay.

333
00:23:43,213 --> 00:23:45,215
That's William?

334
00:23:45,299 --> 00:23:48,886
William Sean McFly, the first of
our family to be born in America.

335
00:23:49,845 --> 00:23:51,221
It's okay, Will.

336
00:23:51,305 --> 00:23:54,892
This here is Mr. Clint
Eastwood here visiting.

337
00:23:54,975 --> 00:23:57,019
He surely likes you,
Mr. Eastwood.

338
00:23:58,020 --> 00:23:59,313
Maggie.

339
00:24:01,607 --> 00:24:02,775
I've got supper.

340
00:24:02,858 --> 00:24:05,486
I'm not one to pry
into a man's personal affairs,

341
00:24:05,569 --> 00:24:09,990
but exactly how is it that
you came to be way out here

342
00:24:10,074 --> 00:24:14,411
without a horse,
or boots, or a hat?

343
00:24:14,495 --> 00:24:16,705
Well, my car...

344
00:24:16,789 --> 00:24:19,917
Horse broke down,
and a bear ate my boots,

345
00:24:20,000 --> 00:24:21,877
and I guess
I just forgot my hat.

346
00:24:21,961 --> 00:24:24,755
How could you forget
a thing like your hat?

347
00:24:24,838 --> 00:24:26,048
Would you like
some water?

348
00:24:26,131 --> 00:24:27,299
Yeah.

349
00:24:38,143 --> 00:24:40,479
I'll tell you what
I'll do, Mr. Eastwood.

350
00:24:40,562 --> 00:24:42,898
I'll help you find
your blacksmith friend.

351
00:24:44,233 --> 00:24:47,319
You can stay
the night in the barn,

352
00:24:47,403 --> 00:24:50,572
and tomorrow I'll take you
as far as the railroad tracks.

353
00:24:50,656 --> 00:24:52,950
You can follow them
straight on into town.

354
00:24:55,953 --> 00:24:57,621
I'll even
give you a hat.

355
00:25:00,958 --> 00:25:02,334
That's great. Thanks.

356
00:25:05,754 --> 00:25:07,297
Ah...

357
00:25:07,381 --> 00:25:08,424
That's my William.

358
00:25:12,803 --> 00:25:14,096
Oh.

359
00:25:15,431 --> 00:25:16,557
Yes.

360
00:25:17,516 --> 00:25:20,102
I think you'll find
the barn comfortable.

361
00:25:20,185 --> 00:25:23,105
Never had any complaints
about it from the pigs.

362
00:25:23,188 --> 00:25:25,190
- Mmm.
- Seamus!

363
00:25:25,274 --> 00:25:26,358
A word with you.

364
00:25:26,442 --> 00:25:27,443
Aye.

365
00:25:31,155 --> 00:25:33,532
Will you hold him
for a minute?

366
00:25:37,286 --> 00:25:39,597
Are you sure you're not after
bringing a curse on this house

367
00:25:39,621 --> 00:25:41,832
taking him in like that?

368
00:25:41,915 --> 00:25:43,333
He's such
a strange young man.

369
00:25:43,417 --> 00:25:46,378
Aye. But I've just got a
feeling about him, Maggie,

370
00:25:46,462 --> 00:25:49,173
that looking after him
is the right thing to do.

371
00:25:49,256 --> 00:25:50,674
That it's important.

372
00:25:50,758 --> 00:25:52,426
Hey, buddy.

373
00:25:52,509 --> 00:25:54,636
Look how the baby
takes to him.

374
00:25:54,720 --> 00:25:58,390
Little Will never
takes to strangers.

375
00:25:58,474 --> 00:26:01,393
It's almost as if
he's connected to us.

376
00:26:01,477 --> 00:26:02,478
Hey, Will.

377
00:26:03,103 --> 00:26:05,773
So you're my
great-grandfather.

378
00:26:05,856 --> 00:26:08,233
The first McFly
born in America.

379
00:26:13,989 --> 00:26:15,783
And you peed on me.

380
00:27:06,708 --> 00:27:07,918
Give us some soap.

381
00:27:08,001 --> 00:27:09,169
Here you go.

382
00:28:56,318 --> 00:28:58,946
Take a look and see what
just breezed in the door.

383
00:29:01,865 --> 00:29:04,368
Why, I didn't know
the circus was in town.

384
00:29:04,451 --> 00:29:07,996
Must have got that
shirt off a dead Chinese.

385
00:29:28,100 --> 00:29:29,685
What will it be,
stranger?

386
00:29:32,271 --> 00:29:35,899
I'll have ice water.

387
00:29:35,983 --> 00:29:37,651
Ice water?

388
00:29:38,694 --> 00:29:39,695
Water?

389
00:29:39,778 --> 00:29:41,863
You want water, you
better go dunk your head

390
00:29:41,947 --> 00:29:44,533
in the horse trough out
there.

391
00:29:44,616 --> 00:29:47,703
In here,
we pour whiskey.

392
00:29:58,505 --> 00:29:59,756
Excuse me.

393
00:29:59,840 --> 00:30:01,049
For what?

394
00:30:01,133 --> 00:30:02,591
I'm trying to
find the blacksmith.

395
00:30:02,592 --> 00:30:03,912
I'm trying to
find the blacksmith.

396
00:30:04,594 --> 00:30:06,054
Hey, McFly!

397
00:30:08,056 --> 00:30:10,809
Thought I done told
you never to come in...

398
00:30:12,561 --> 00:30:14,354
You ain't
Seamus McFly.

399
00:30:16,064 --> 00:30:17,733
You look like him,
though,

400
00:30:17,816 --> 00:30:19,401
especially with
that dog-ugly hat.

401
00:30:22,738 --> 00:30:25,073
You kin to
that hay barber?

402
00:30:25,157 --> 00:30:26,950
What's your name,
dude?

403
00:30:27,034 --> 00:30:28,327
Marty...

404
00:30:29,453 --> 00:30:30,746
Eastwood.

405
00:30:32,205 --> 00:30:33,707
Clint Eastwood.

406
00:30:35,584 --> 00:30:37,919
What kind of
stupid name is that?

407
00:30:38,003 --> 00:30:40,088
I'd say he's the
runt of the litter.

408
00:30:40,172 --> 00:30:42,799
Hey, now, boys, would you take
a look-see at these pearly whites?

409
00:30:42,883 --> 00:30:44,235
Hell, I ain't seen
teeth look that straight

410
00:30:44,259 --> 00:30:45,302
that weren't
store-bought.

411
00:30:45,385 --> 00:30:47,346
Take a gander at
them moccasins.

412
00:30:47,429 --> 00:30:48,930
What kind of skins
is them?

413
00:30:49,014 --> 00:30:50,098
What's that
writing mean?

414
00:30:50,182 --> 00:30:51,516
"Nike"?
What is that?

415
00:30:51,600 --> 00:30:53,226
Some sort of In jun
talk or something?

416
00:30:56,438 --> 00:31:00,067
Bartender, I'm looking for that
no-good, cheating blacksmith.

417
00:31:00,150 --> 00:31:01,485
You seen him?

418
00:31:01,568 --> 00:31:03,362
No, sir, Mr. Tannen.
I have not.

419
00:31:04,905 --> 00:31:06,198
Tannen.

420
00:31:07,115 --> 00:31:08,450
You're "Mad Dog" Tannen.

421
00:31:14,831 --> 00:31:16,625
"Mad Dog"?

422
00:31:16,708 --> 00:31:18,043
I hate that name.

423
00:31:19,044 --> 00:31:21,671
I hate it, you hear?

424
00:31:21,755 --> 00:31:24,633
Nobody calls me
"Mad Dog"!

425
00:31:24,716 --> 00:31:28,637
Especially not some duded-up,
egg-sucking gutter trash.

426
00:31:30,806 --> 00:31:32,682
Dance!

427
00:31:32,766 --> 00:31:33,892
Come on!

428
00:31:36,103 --> 00:31:39,022
Come on, runt! You can
dance better than that!

429
00:31:49,282 --> 00:31:50,325
Shit.

430
00:31:51,576 --> 00:31:52,744
Whoo!

431
00:32:12,180 --> 00:32:13,765
You better run,
squirrel.

432
00:32:13,849 --> 00:32:15,225
Get him!

433
00:32:27,154 --> 00:32:28,321
Whoa!

434
00:33:18,914 --> 00:33:21,791
We got ourselves
a new courthouse.

435
00:33:21,875 --> 00:33:23,418
High time
we had a hanging!

436
00:33:43,480 --> 00:33:45,190
Look at him swing.

437
00:33:48,652 --> 00:33:51,947
Yeah, haven't
had a hanging in a long time!

438
00:33:55,158 --> 00:33:58,495
It'll shoot the fleas off a dog's
back at 500 yards, Tannen,

439
00:33:58,578 --> 00:34:00,580
and it's pointed
straight at your head!

440
00:34:11,091 --> 00:34:12,551
You owe me money,
blacksmith.

441
00:34:12,634 --> 00:34:14,052
How do you figure?

442
00:34:14,135 --> 00:34:15,762
My horse threw a shoe.

443
00:34:15,845 --> 00:34:17,448
And seeing as you was the
one that done the shoeing,

444
00:34:17,472 --> 00:34:18,848
I say that makes
you responsible.

445
00:34:18,932 --> 00:34:22,894
Well, since you never paid me for
the job, I say that makes us even.

446
00:34:22,978 --> 00:34:25,063
Wrong! See I was
on my horse

447
00:34:25,146 --> 00:34:26,916
when it threw the shoe,
and I got throwed off!

448
00:34:26,940 --> 00:34:29,067
And that caused me
to bust a perfectly

449
00:34:29,150 --> 00:34:31,570
good bottle of
fine Kentucky red eye.

450
00:34:31,653 --> 00:34:33,530
So the way
I figure it, blacksmith,

451
00:34:33,613 --> 00:34:35,490
you owe me $5
for the whiskey

452
00:34:35,574 --> 00:34:37,784
and $75 for the horse!

453
00:34:38,660 --> 00:34:39,744
That's the $80.

454
00:34:39,828 --> 00:34:42,289
Look, if your horse
threw a shoe,

455
00:34:42,372 --> 00:34:44,249
bring him back,
and I'll reshoe him.

456
00:34:44,332 --> 00:34:45,959
I done shot that horse!

457
00:34:46,042 --> 00:34:48,253
Well, that's your problem,
Tannen.

458
00:34:48,336 --> 00:34:51,381
Wrong. That's yours.

459
00:34:51,464 --> 00:34:55,135
So from now on, you better be
looking behind you when you walk.

460
00:34:55,218 --> 00:34:57,846
Because one day you're
gonna get a bullet in your back.

461
00:35:07,856 --> 00:35:08,856
Doc!

462
00:35:08,898 --> 00:35:14,029
Marty, I gave you explicit
instructions not to come here

463
00:35:14,112 --> 00:35:16,281
but to go
directly back to 1985.

464
00:35:16,364 --> 00:35:19,034
I know, Doc,
but I had to come.

465
00:35:19,868 --> 00:35:22,287
But it's good to
see you, Marty.

466
00:35:26,541 --> 00:35:28,561
Marty, you're gonna have to do
something about those clothes.

467
00:35:28,585 --> 00:35:30,021
You walk around town
dressed like that,

468
00:35:30,045 --> 00:35:31,588
you're liable
to get shot.

469
00:35:31,671 --> 00:35:32,671
Or hanged.

470
00:35:32,714 --> 00:35:35,842
What idiot dressed you
in that outfit?

471
00:35:35,925 --> 00:35:37,218
You did.

472
00:35:40,055 --> 00:35:42,307
"Shot in the back
by Buford Tannen

473
00:35:42,682 --> 00:35:44,643
"over a matter of $80,

474
00:35:44,809 --> 00:35:46,978
"September 7"?

475
00:35:47,062 --> 00:35:49,064
That's this Monday!

476
00:35:49,147 --> 00:35:51,650
Now I wish
I'd paid him off.

477
00:35:51,733 --> 00:35:53,485
And who's this
beloved Clara?

478
00:35:53,568 --> 00:35:55,862
I don't know
anyone named Clara.

479
00:35:55,945 --> 00:35:58,740
I don't know, Doc.

480
00:35:58,823 --> 00:36:00,992
I thought maybe she
was a girlfriend of yours.

481
00:36:01,076 --> 00:36:06,331
Marty, my involvement in such
a social relationship here in 1885

482
00:36:06,414 --> 00:36:10,335
could result in a disruption
of the space-time continuum.

483
00:36:10,418 --> 00:36:14,464
As a scientist, I can
never take that risk.

484
00:36:16,091 --> 00:36:18,635
Certainly not after what
we've already been through.

485
00:36:18,718 --> 00:36:19,844
Emmett!

486
00:36:19,928 --> 00:36:21,096
Hello, Emmett.

487
00:36:21,179 --> 00:36:22,305
Hubert.

488
00:36:22,847 --> 00:36:24,057
It's the mayor.

489
00:36:24,140 --> 00:36:26,351
Excuse me, Emmett.

490
00:36:26,434 --> 00:36:29,104
Do you remember last week at the
town meeting when you volunteered

491
00:36:29,187 --> 00:36:30,522
to meet the new
schoolteacher

492
00:36:30,605 --> 00:36:31,707
at the station
when she came in?

493
00:36:31,731 --> 00:36:32,731
Oh, yes, quite so.

494
00:36:32,774 --> 00:36:35,235
Well, we just got word
she's coming in tomorrow.

495
00:36:35,318 --> 00:36:37,278
Here are the details
for you.

496
00:36:37,362 --> 00:36:38,822
Thanks for
all your help.

497
00:36:38,905 --> 00:36:40,824
Anytime, Hubert.

498
00:36:40,907 --> 00:36:44,953
Oh! Her name's Miss
Clayton. Clara Clayton.

499
00:36:53,461 --> 00:36:57,632
Well, Doc, now we
know who Clara is.

500
00:36:59,384 --> 00:37:01,594
Marty, it's impossible.

501
00:37:01,678 --> 00:37:04,973
The idea that I could fall in love
at first sight is romantic nonsense.

502
00:37:06,141 --> 00:37:08,393
There's no scientific
rationale for that concept.

503
00:37:08,476 --> 00:37:10,478
Aw, come on, Doc.
It's not science.

504
00:37:10,562 --> 00:37:13,064
You meet the right girl, it
just hits you. It's like lightning.

505
00:37:13,148 --> 00:37:14,733
Marty, please
don't say that.

506
00:37:14,816 --> 00:37:17,402
That's the way it was
for me and Jennifer.

507
00:37:17,485 --> 00:37:20,447
I mean, we couldn't keep
our eyes off each other.

508
00:37:20,530 --> 00:37:22,574
God, Jennifer, damn. I
hope she's all right, Doc.

509
00:37:22,657 --> 00:37:24,993
I can't believe we just
left her there on the porch.

510
00:37:25,076 --> 00:37:27,078
Don't worry, Marty.
She'll be fine.

511
00:37:27,162 --> 00:37:29,247
When you burned
the almanac in 1955,

512
00:37:29,330 --> 00:37:30,915
the normal timeline
was restored.

513
00:37:30,999 --> 00:37:32,751
That means once
we're back in 1985,

514
00:37:32,834 --> 00:37:34,914
you just have to go over to
her house to wake her up.

515
00:37:36,713 --> 00:37:39,007
Oh, Marty, turn
that valve over there

516
00:37:39,090 --> 00:37:40,133
all the way
to the right.

517
00:37:40,216 --> 00:37:42,093
Yeah, pull it
all the way around.

518
00:37:42,177 --> 00:37:43,678
Okay. Let's go!

519
00:37:57,859 --> 00:37:59,402
Iced tea?

520
00:37:59,486 --> 00:38:00,862
No, thanks.

521
00:38:01,446 --> 00:38:03,323
It's a refrigerator.

522
00:38:03,406 --> 00:38:05,116
Well, I guess
Miss Clayton

523
00:38:05,200 --> 00:38:07,118
will have to find
other transportation.

524
00:38:07,202 --> 00:38:08,203
If I never
meet the woman,

525
00:38:08,286 --> 00:38:10,486
there's no possibility of a
romantic infatuation, right?

526
00:38:10,538 --> 00:38:12,415
You're the doc, Doc.
All right.

527
00:38:12,499 --> 00:38:15,043
We'll get the DeLorean and
get ourselves back to the future.

528
00:38:15,126 --> 00:38:16,461
Oh, listen, Doc,
I tore a hole

529
00:38:16,544 --> 00:38:17,772
in the gas tank
when I was landing,

530
00:38:17,796 --> 00:38:20,381
SO we're gonna have to
patch it up and get some gas.

531
00:38:20,465 --> 00:38:22,759
You mean we're
out of gas?

532
00:38:22,842 --> 00:38:23,843
Yeah, it's no big deal.

533
00:38:23,927 --> 00:38:25,553
We've got
Mr. Fusion, right?

534
00:38:25,637 --> 00:38:29,474
Mr. Fusion powers the time
circuits and the Flux Capacitor,

535
00:38:29,557 --> 00:38:32,477
but the internal combustion
engine runs on ordinary gasoline.

536
00:38:32,560 --> 00:38:34,312
It always has.

537
00:38:34,395 --> 00:38:36,356
There's not gonna be
a gas station around here

538
00:38:36,439 --> 00:38:39,067
until sometime in
the next century.

539
00:38:39,150 --> 00:38:40,819
Without gasoline,

540
00:38:40,902 --> 00:38:44,072
we can't get the Delorean
up to 88 miles per hour.

541
00:38:46,616 --> 00:38:48,034
So what do we do?

542
00:38:58,503 --> 00:39:00,088
Twenty-four!

543
00:39:00,171 --> 00:39:01,714
It's no use, Marty!

544
00:39:01,798 --> 00:39:04,008
Even the fastest
horse in the world

545
00:39:04,092 --> 00:39:06,761
won't run more than 35,
40 miles an hour!

546
00:39:17,438 --> 00:39:20,608
Bartender said that's
the strongest stuff they got.

547
00:39:20,692 --> 00:39:22,235
Try it, Marty.

548
00:39:33,496 --> 00:39:35,290
Give it more gas!

549
00:39:44,591 --> 00:39:45,800
Damn!

550
00:39:47,802 --> 00:39:50,388
It blew the fuel
injection manifold.

551
00:39:50,471 --> 00:39:52,390
Strong stuff,
all right.

552
00:39:52,473 --> 00:39:55,393
It will take me a month
to rebuild it.

553
00:39:55,476 --> 00:39:56,853
A month?

554
00:39:57,186 --> 00:39:58,980
Doc, you're gonna
get shot on Monday!

555
00:39:59,063 --> 00:40:02,609
I know! I know!
I know! I wish...

556
00:40:02,692 --> 00:40:07,405
Wait. I've got it! We can
simply roll it down a steep hill!

557
00:40:07,488 --> 00:40:09,407
Oh! We'd never find
a smooth enough surface.

558
00:40:09,490 --> 00:40:10,491
Unless...

559
00:40:10,575 --> 00:40:12,535
Of course! Ice!

560
00:40:12,619 --> 00:40:14,829
We'll wait until winter
when the lake freezes over.

561
00:40:14,913 --> 00:40:16,664
Winter? Doc, what
are you talking about?

562
00:40:16,748 --> 00:40:18,207
Monday!
It's three days away!

563
00:40:18,291 --> 00:40:19,310
All right, all right,
all right.

564
00:40:19,334 --> 00:40:22,587
Just let's think this
thing through logically.

565
00:40:22,670 --> 00:40:24,422
We know it won't run
under its own power.

566
00:40:24,505 --> 00:40:26,382
We know
we can't pull it.

567
00:40:26,466 --> 00:40:29,928
But if we could
figure out a way

568
00:40:30,011 --> 00:40:33,181
to push it up to
88 miles an hour.

569
00:40:33,264 --> 00:40:34,641
Huh?

570
00:40:42,815 --> 00:40:43,900
That's it.

571
00:40:43,983 --> 00:40:47,862
How fast can she go? Why,
I've had her up to 55 myself.

572
00:40:49,030 --> 00:40:51,032
I heard that
Fearless Frank Fargo

573
00:40:51,115 --> 00:40:52,450
got one of these
up to near 70

574
00:40:52,533 --> 00:40:54,327
out past
Verde Junction.

575
00:40:54,410 --> 00:40:57,080
Do you think it's
possible to get it up to 90?

576
00:40:57,163 --> 00:40:58,414
Ninety?

577
00:40:58,498 --> 00:41:01,292
Tarnation, son, who'd ever
need to be in such a hurry?

578
00:41:01,376 --> 00:41:03,628
It's just a little bet he
and I have, that's all.

579
00:41:03,711 --> 00:41:06,214
Theoretically speaking,
could it be done?

580
00:41:06,297 --> 00:41:08,132
Well, I suppose,
if you had a straight

581
00:41:08,216 --> 00:41:10,051
stretch of track
with a level grade,

582
00:41:10,134 --> 00:41:12,595
and you weren't hauling
no cars behind you,

583
00:41:12,679 --> 00:41:14,472
and if you could get
the fire hot enough.

584
00:41:14,555 --> 00:41:18,518
And I'm talking about hotter than
the blazes of hell and damnation itself.

585
00:41:18,601 --> 00:41:20,871
Then yes, sir, it might be
possible to get her up that fast.

586
00:41:20,895 --> 00:41:22,981
Tell me, when does the
next train come through here?

587
00:41:23,064 --> 00:41:24,482
Monday morning at 8:00.

588
00:41:24,565 --> 00:41:27,151
Here. This spur that
runs off the main line

589
00:41:27,235 --> 00:41:28,736
three miles out
to Clayton Ravine.

590
00:41:28,820 --> 00:41:30,363
It's a long stretch
of level track

591
00:41:30,446 --> 00:41:32,782
that will still
exist in 1985.

592
00:41:32,865 --> 00:41:36,327
This is where we'll push the
Delorean with the locomotive.

593
00:41:36,411 --> 00:41:39,956
Funny. This map calls Clayton
Ravine Shonash Ravine.

594
00:41:40,039 --> 00:41:43,167
That must be the old
Indian name for it. It's perfect.

595
00:41:43,251 --> 00:41:45,044
A nice long run
that goes clear across

596
00:41:45,128 --> 00:41:46,170
the bridge
over the ravine,

597
00:41:46,254 --> 00:41:48,589
you know, over near that
Hilldale housing development.

598
00:41:48,673 --> 00:41:53,261
Right, Doc, but according to
this map, there is no bridge.

599
00:41:53,636 --> 00:41:57,181
Well, Doc,
we can scratch that idea.

600
00:41:57,265 --> 00:41:58,659
I mean, we can't
wait a year and a half

601
00:41:58,683 --> 00:41:59,785
for this thing
to get finished.

602
00:41:59,809 --> 00:42:01,227
Marty, it's perfect.

603
00:42:01,310 --> 00:42:03,438
You're just not thinking
fourth-dimensionally.

604
00:42:03,521 --> 00:42:05,290
Right. Right. I have a
real problem with that.

605
00:42:05,314 --> 00:42:09,193
Don't you see? The
bridge will exist in 1985.

606
00:42:09,277 --> 00:42:11,154
It's safe
and still in use.

607
00:42:11,237 --> 00:42:14,032
Therefore, as long as we get the
DeLorean up to 88 miles an hour

608
00:42:14,115 --> 00:42:15,825
before we hit
the edge of the ravine,

609
00:42:15,908 --> 00:42:18,369
we'll instantaneously
arrive at a point in time

610
00:42:18,453 --> 00:42:20,580
where the bridge
is completed.

611
00:42:20,663 --> 00:42:26,210
We'll have track under us and
coast safely across the ravine.

612
00:42:26,294 --> 00:42:27,462
What about
the locomotive?

613
00:42:27,545 --> 00:42:29,547
It will be
a spectacular wreck.

614
00:42:29,630 --> 00:42:31,507
Too bad no one will
be around to see it.

615
00:42:33,342 --> 00:42:35,219
Help me! Help!

616
00:42:35,303 --> 00:42:36,679
Great Scott!

617
00:43:19,180 --> 00:43:20,264
Hurry!

618
00:43:22,391 --> 00:43:23,684
Jump!

619
00:43:33,986 --> 00:43:34,987
Whoa.

620
00:43:35,822 --> 00:43:36,989
Whoa.

621
00:43:37,365 --> 00:43:38,407
Oh...

622
00:43:38,491 --> 00:43:41,202
Thank you, sir.
You saved my...

623
00:43:43,454 --> 00:43:44,497
... life.

624
00:43:48,876 --> 00:43:51,295
Emmett Brown at
your service, Miss...

625
00:43:54,757 --> 00:43:56,092
Clayton.

626
00:43:57,718 --> 00:43:58,886
Clara Clayton.

627
00:44:00,221 --> 00:44:01,222
Clara!

628
00:44:05,059 --> 00:44:06,769
What a beautiful name.

629
00:44:24,245 --> 00:44:26,914
May I help you
inside with these?

630
00:44:26,998 --> 00:44:29,709
Oh, no, that won't be
necessary. I can take care of it.

631
00:44:29,792 --> 00:44:32,295
You've done more
than enough already.

632
00:44:32,378 --> 00:44:34,130
But it's really
no trouble.

633
00:44:34,213 --> 00:44:36,591
Doc, she says it's fine,
and we got to get going.

634
00:44:36,674 --> 00:44:40,428
Ma'am, good luck with your
school teaching and everything.

635
00:44:40,511 --> 00:44:43,514
Clara, I'll straighten
everything out

636
00:44:43,598 --> 00:44:45,391
with Mr. Statler for
the buckboard rental.

637
00:44:45,474 --> 00:44:46,809
Don't worry about that.

638
00:44:46,893 --> 00:44:48,936
I feel somewhat responsible
for what happened.

639
00:44:49,020 --> 00:44:50,021
Oh.

640
00:44:50,938 --> 00:44:54,692
That would be very gentlemanly
of you, Mr. Brown. Emmett.

641
00:44:57,570 --> 00:45:00,031
You know,
I'm almost glad

642
00:45:00,114 --> 00:45:01,490
that snake
spooked those horses.

643
00:45:01,574 --> 00:45:04,493
Otherwise,
we might never have met.

644
00:45:04,577 --> 00:45:06,078
I suppose
it was destiny.

645
00:45:09,624 --> 00:45:11,792
Well, thank you
for everything.

646
00:45:12,210 --> 00:45:14,086
You're quite welcome.

647
00:45:16,422 --> 00:45:19,050
I will see you again,
won't I?

648
00:45:19,133 --> 00:45:20,413
Of course.
You'll see lots of me.

649
00:45:20,468 --> 00:45:21,928
I'm sure.
I have a shop in town.

650
00:45:22,011 --> 00:45:24,889
I'm the local scientist,
blacksmith.

651
00:45:25,556 --> 00:45:28,309
Science?
What sort of science?

652
00:45:28,392 --> 00:45:30,144
Astronomy?
Chemistry?

653
00:45:30,603 --> 00:45:33,064
Actually, I'm a student
of all sciences.

654
00:45:33,147 --> 00:45:36,192
Hey, Doc,
we got to get going.

655
00:45:36,275 --> 00:45:39,237
Oh, yes.
Well, excuse us, Clara.

656
00:45:39,320 --> 00:45:41,155
We have to get going.

657
00:45:45,743 --> 00:45:46,827
Toodle-oo.

658
00:45:55,211 --> 00:45:57,231
What do you mean, you're
gonna be seeing lots of her, Doc?

659
00:45:57,255 --> 00:46:00,424
Well, I might see her
again just in passing.

660
00:46:00,508 --> 00:46:02,748
Come on, Doc. Did you see
the way she was looking at you?

661
00:46:04,595 --> 00:46:07,682
She did have
quite a scare, right?

662
00:46:07,765 --> 00:46:09,284
After all, Miss Clayton
almost ended up

663
00:46:09,308 --> 00:46:11,227
at the bottom of
Clayton Ravine.

664
00:46:11,310 --> 00:46:12,310
Clayton Ravine.

665
00:46:12,353 --> 00:46:17,483
Holy shit. Hey, Doc, Clayton
Ravine was named after a teacher.

666
00:46:17,566 --> 00:46:19,527
They say she fell in
there 100 years ago.

667
00:46:19,610 --> 00:46:22,446
100 years ago!
That's this year!

668
00:46:22,530 --> 00:46:23,840
Every kid in school
knows that story,

669
00:46:23,864 --> 00:46:24,883
"cause we all
have teachers

670
00:46:24,907 --> 00:46:26,909
we'd like to see
fall into the ravine.

671
00:46:26,993 --> 00:46:28,452
Great Scott!

672
00:46:29,829 --> 00:46:33,040
Then she was supposed
to go over in that wagon.

673
00:46:34,208 --> 00:46:37,086
Now I may have
seriously altered history.

674
00:46:38,212 --> 00:46:41,257
Look, Doc, what's the
worst that can happen, huh?

675
00:46:41,340 --> 00:46:43,718
So they don't name
the ravine after her.

676
00:46:43,801 --> 00:46:44,987
Let's just get
the DeLorean ready

677
00:46:45,011 --> 00:46:46,137
and get the hell
out of here.

678
00:46:46,220 --> 00:46:49,682
I wish I'd never invented
that infernal time machine.

679
00:46:51,017 --> 00:46:53,769
It's caused
nothing but disaster.

680
00:46:57,398 --> 00:46:58,649
Doc, Doc,
this is Marty.

681
00:46:58,733 --> 00:46:59,817
Do you read me? Over.

682
00:46:59,900 --> 00:47:00,943
Check, Marty.

683
00:47:01,027 --> 00:47:03,404
Great, Doc.
These things still work.

684
00:47:03,487 --> 00:47:04,905
All right, Marty.

685
00:47:04,989 --> 00:47:08,451
Once more, let's go over
the entire plan and layout.

686
00:47:08,534 --> 00:47:10,595
I apologize for the crudity
of this model, but I just...

687
00:47:10,619 --> 00:47:13,622
Yeah, I know, Doc. It's
not to scale. It's okay, Doc.

688
00:47:13,706 --> 00:47:17,168
All right.
Tomorrow night, Sunday,

689
00:47:17,251 --> 00:47:20,129
we'll load the Delorean onto
the tracks here, on the spur

690
00:47:20,212 --> 00:47:22,506
right by the old
abandoned silver mine.

691
00:47:22,590 --> 00:47:25,426
The switch track is where
the spur runs off the main line

692
00:47:25,509 --> 00:47:29,013
three miles out to
Clayton... Shonash Ravine.

693
00:47:29,096 --> 00:47:31,891
The train leaves the station
at 8:00 Monday morning.

694
00:47:31,974 --> 00:47:34,518
We'll stop it here, uncouple
the cars from the tender,

695
00:47:34,602 --> 00:47:36,812
throw the switch track,
and then we'll hijack...

696
00:47:36,896 --> 00:47:40,858
Borrow the locomotive and
use it to push the time machine.

697
00:47:40,941 --> 00:47:42,151
According to
my calculations,

698
00:47:42,234 --> 00:47:43,611
we'll reach
88 miles per hour

699
00:47:43,694 --> 00:47:45,696
just before we hit
the edge of the ravine,

700
00:47:45,780 --> 00:47:49,450
at which point we'll be
instantaneously transported back to 1985

701
00:47:49,533 --> 00:47:51,952
and coast safely across
the completed bridge.

702
00:47:52,036 --> 00:47:54,789
What does this mean?
"Point of no return."

703
00:47:54,872 --> 00:47:56,832
That's our
fail-safe point.

704
00:47:56,916 --> 00:47:58,352
Up until there, we have
still have enough time

705
00:47:58,376 --> 00:48:00,961
to stop the locomotive before
it plunges into the ravine.

706
00:48:01,045 --> 00:48:02,245
But once we
pass this windmill,

707
00:48:02,296 --> 00:48:03,464
it's the future
or bust.

708
00:48:06,467 --> 00:48:10,137
Here you go, Marty. Connect
that to the positive terminal.

709
00:48:11,138 --> 00:48:12,240
All right,
Marty, you all set?

710
00:48:12,264 --> 00:48:13,516
Yeah. Yeah. Go!

711
00:48:13,974 --> 00:48:16,644
Train pulling out of the station!

712
00:48:16,727 --> 00:48:18,896
Coming up to
the switch track!

713
00:48:18,979 --> 00:48:21,565
Stop at
the switch track!

714
00:48:21,649 --> 00:48:23,901
Throw the switch!

715
00:48:23,984 --> 00:48:26,404
Pull up to the DeLorean!

716
00:48:26,487 --> 00:48:30,658
Pushing the DeLorean
up to 88 miles per hour!

717
00:48:37,039 --> 00:48:38,999
It couldn't be simpler.

718
00:48:45,506 --> 00:48:47,508
Emmett?

719
00:48:47,591 --> 00:48:49,176
It's Clara.

720
00:48:49,260 --> 00:48:51,345
Quick, cover the DeLorean!

721
00:48:58,644 --> 00:48:59,645
Hello.

722
00:48:59,728 --> 00:49:00,938
Why, hello.

723
00:49:01,021 --> 00:49:03,149
This is
quite a surprise.

724
00:49:04,692 --> 00:49:06,819
Well, I hope I'm not
disturbing anything.

725
00:49:06,902 --> 00:49:10,448
Oh, no, no, we were just
doing a little model railroading.

726
00:49:10,990 --> 00:49:12,074
Oh.

727
00:49:13,576 --> 00:49:16,620
Emmett, when my bags
were thrown from the wagon,

728
00:49:16,704 --> 00:49:18,581
my telescope
was damaged.

729
00:49:18,664 --> 00:49:22,209
And since you mentioned
an interest in science,

730
00:49:22,293 --> 00:49:25,629
I thought you might be
able to repair it for me.

731
00:49:25,713 --> 00:49:27,673
I would pay you,
of course.

732
00:49:27,756 --> 00:49:32,052
Oh, no, no, no. I wouldn't
think of charging you for this.

733
00:49:32,136 --> 00:49:34,305
Well, let's have
a look at it.

734
00:49:34,388 --> 00:49:36,640
I think a lens may
be out of alignment,

735
00:49:36,724 --> 00:49:40,269
because if you
move it this way,

736
00:49:40,352 --> 00:49:43,397
the image turns fuzzy.
See?

737
00:49:43,481 --> 00:49:48,569
But if you turn it
the other way then...

738
00:49:49,528 --> 00:49:51,322
Everything becomes...

739
00:49:54,241 --> 00:49:55,576
...Clear.

740
00:50:00,748 --> 00:50:03,501
I could repair it right away
and have it for you tonight.

741
00:50:03,584 --> 00:50:06,337
Oh, tonight is
the town festival.

742
00:50:06,420 --> 00:50:09,006
I wouldn't dream of having
you work on my telescope

743
00:50:09,089 --> 00:50:10,841
during such
an important occasion.

744
00:50:10,925 --> 00:50:14,094
You are planning on
attending, aren't you?

745
00:50:15,346 --> 00:50:17,014
Well, actually,
ma'am, I don't think...

746
00:50:17,097 --> 00:50:19,683
Yes, of course,
the festival.

747
00:50:19,767 --> 00:50:21,685
Well, in that case,

748
00:50:21,769 --> 00:50:25,314
I'll see you this evening
at the festival, Emmett.

749
00:50:25,397 --> 00:50:27,358
Mr. Eastwood.
Ma'am.

750
00:50:30,444 --> 00:50:32,863
Thank you for taking
care of my telescope.

751
00:50:32,947 --> 00:50:34,823
You're quite welcome.

752
00:50:40,788 --> 00:50:42,206
It's a nice telescope.

753
00:50:42,289 --> 00:50:42,331
Ladies and gentlemen,

754
00:50:42,332 --> 00:50:44,959
Ladies and gentlemen,

755
00:50:45,042 --> 00:50:49,421
as mayor of Hill Valley,
it gives me great pleasure

756
00:50:49,505 --> 00:50:52,633
to dedicate this clock to
the people of Hill County!

757
00:50:53,801 --> 00:50:57,555
May it stand
for all time!

758
00:50:57,638 --> 00:50:58,931
Tell me when, gentlemen.

759
00:51:00,140 --> 00:51:03,978
Three! Two! One!

760
00:51:04,812 --> 00:51:06,146
Now!

761
00:51:11,610 --> 00:51:14,071
Let the festivities
begin!

762
00:51:21,704 --> 00:51:22,889
You know, Marty,
in a way, it's fitting

763
00:51:22,913 --> 00:51:25,207
that you and I are
here to witness this.

764
00:51:25,291 --> 00:51:27,960
It's too bad I didn't
bring my camera.

765
00:51:34,675 --> 00:51:35,801
Ready, gentlemen?

766
00:51:37,177 --> 00:51:40,598
The only problem is we'll never
be able to show it to anybody.

767
00:51:40,681 --> 00:51:41,849
Smile, Doc.

768
00:51:44,268 --> 00:51:45,519
Yee-ha!

769
00:52:06,624 --> 00:52:08,959
What great music!

770
00:52:09,043 --> 00:52:11,754
Yeah, it's got a beat,
and you can dance to it.

771
00:52:11,837 --> 00:52:12,838
Step right up
gentlemen

772
00:52:12,921 --> 00:52:14,840
and test your mettle
with the latest products

773
00:52:14,923 --> 00:52:18,969
from Colonel Samuel Colt's Patent
Firearms Manufacturing Company

774
00:52:19,053 --> 00:52:20,679
of Hartford,
Connecticut.

775
00:52:20,763 --> 00:52:23,682
Now, take
this model, for example.

776
00:52:23,766 --> 00:52:27,436
The new, improved and
refined Colt Peacemaker

777
00:52:27,519 --> 00:52:32,733
selling to you tonight for
the low, low price of $12.

778
00:52:48,874 --> 00:52:50,834
Good evening.
Evening.

779
00:52:52,086 --> 00:52:54,505
You look very nice.

780
00:52:54,588 --> 00:52:56,048
Thank you.

781
00:53:04,932 --> 00:53:06,558
Would you like...

782
00:53:08,435 --> 00:53:09,645
Would you care to...

783
00:53:09,728 --> 00:53:10,938
I'd love to.

784
00:53:29,289 --> 00:53:31,309
Young man, how about you?
You want to give it a try?

785
00:53:31,333 --> 00:53:34,545
No, no. No, thanks.
Hey, Doc, this...

786
00:53:42,428 --> 00:53:43,637
Son!

787
00:53:44,471 --> 00:53:45,472
Sonny boy!

788
00:53:45,556 --> 00:53:47,015
The Doc can dance?

789
00:53:49,643 --> 00:53:53,522
Son. Son. Son!

790
00:53:53,605 --> 00:53:58,026
Hey, I just told you that even a
baby could handle this weapon.

791
00:53:58,110 --> 00:54:01,196
Surely you're not afraid to try
something that a baby could do.

792
00:54:01,280 --> 00:54:02,531
Hey, I'm not afraid
of nothing.

793
00:54:02,614 --> 00:54:05,576
Come on, then. Let's just
step up here like a man.

794
00:54:05,659 --> 00:54:10,080
Now, then what you do IS you
just ease that hammer back there

795
00:54:10,164 --> 00:54:11,915
and squeeze off
a round.

796
00:54:11,999 --> 00:54:13,584
No, no, no!

797
00:54:13,667 --> 00:54:16,420
Right on out there
and be real smooth.

798
00:54:16,503 --> 00:54:18,589
That's the way
you do it.

799
00:54:22,050 --> 00:54:23,719
Listen,
can I try that again?

800
00:54:23,802 --> 00:54:25,220
Yeah, go ahead.

801
00:54:37,357 --> 00:54:39,151
Hey, just tell me
one thing.

802
00:54:39,234 --> 00:54:41,779
Where did you learn
to shoot like that?

803
00:54:41,862 --> 00:54:43,197
7-Eleven.

804
00:54:48,118 --> 00:54:49,411
Buford, you sure
that blacksmith

805
00:54:49,495 --> 00:54:51,288
is gonna be at
this here shindig?

806
00:54:51,371 --> 00:54:54,208
Sure he's here.
Everybody's here tonight.

807
00:54:56,794 --> 00:54:58,629
You gentlemen will have
to check your firearms

808
00:54:58,712 --> 00:55:00,964
if you want to join in
on the festivities.

809
00:55:02,299 --> 00:55:03,801
Who's gonna make us,
tenderfoot? You?

810
00:55:03,884 --> 00:55:05,385
I am.

811
00:55:08,430 --> 00:55:11,183
Marshal Strickland. I
didn't know you was back in town.

812
00:55:11,266 --> 00:55:12,893
If you can't
read the sign, Tannen,

813
00:55:12,976 --> 00:55:15,229
I presume
you can read this.

814
00:55:15,646 --> 00:55:17,040
Pretty tough hombre
when you're pointing

815
00:55:17,064 --> 00:55:18,398
a scatter gun
at a man's back.

816
00:55:18,482 --> 00:55:21,610
Just like you, Tannen, I take
every advantage I can get.

817
00:55:21,693 --> 00:55:24,363
Now you gonna
check your iron?

818
00:55:25,030 --> 00:55:27,491
I was just joking
with your deputy.

819
00:55:27,574 --> 00:55:29,284
Of course,
I'm gonna check my iron.

820
00:55:29,368 --> 00:55:30,744
We all were.
Weren't we, boys?

821
00:55:30,828 --> 00:55:32,162
Yeah, right.

822
00:55:33,789 --> 00:55:35,290
Tannen.

823
00:55:36,416 --> 00:55:38,252
Your knife, too.

824
00:55:45,133 --> 00:55:48,303
Smile, Marshal.
After all, this is a party.

825
00:55:48,387 --> 00:55:50,848
Only party
I'll be smiling at

826
00:55:50,931 --> 00:55:53,600
is the one that sees you
at the end of a rope.

827
00:55:56,520 --> 00:55:57,980
Have fun!

828
00:56:01,650 --> 00:56:03,044
See, that's how
you handle them, Son.

829
00:56:03,068 --> 00:56:04,111
Never give them
an inch

830
00:56:04,194 --> 00:56:06,530
and maintain discipline
at all times.

831
00:56:06,613 --> 00:56:09,533
Remember that word,
discipline.

832
00:56:09,616 --> 00:56:11,118
I will, Pa.

833
00:56:11,952 --> 00:56:14,371
Swing that
partner With a right big swing

834
00:56:14,454 --> 00:56:16,665
Over to the side
It's the same old thing

835
00:56:17,040 --> 00:56:18,083
Thank you very much.

836
00:56:18,166 --> 00:56:22,546
Why, Mr. Eastwood,
nice to see you.

837
00:56:22,629 --> 00:56:27,050
I see you got yourself some
respectable clothes, lad, and a fine hat.

838
00:56:27,134 --> 00:56:28,176
Yeah, well,
a couple people

839
00:56:28,260 --> 00:56:30,470
didn't like the way the
other one looked on me.

840
00:56:30,554 --> 00:56:32,234
Sure, and that one
suits you, Mr. Eastwood.

841
00:56:32,306 --> 00:56:34,141
It's very becoming.
Oh, thanks.

842
00:56:35,350 --> 00:56:38,270
Hey, "Frisbie."
Far out.

843
00:56:41,023 --> 00:56:42,357
What was the meaning
of that?

844
00:56:42,482 --> 00:56:44,067
It was right in
front of him. Aye.

845
00:56:44,151 --> 00:56:46,320
Swing
that partner halfway round

846
00:56:46,403 --> 00:56:48,697
Sashay back to
the head of the set

847
00:56:48,780 --> 00:56:51,742
All the way up to
the head of the set

848
00:56:51,825 --> 00:56:54,578
Now, bow to the partner
You're through

849
00:56:58,832 --> 00:57:00,417
Hey, that's mine!
Not anymore.

850
00:57:00,500 --> 00:57:01,793
Give me that.

851
00:57:01,877 --> 00:57:03,337
There he is, Buford.

852
00:57:04,671 --> 00:57:06,173
Where?
There.

853
00:57:06,256 --> 00:57:08,759
Dancing with that
piece of calico.

854
00:57:08,842 --> 00:57:11,011
What are you
gonna do, boss?

855
00:57:12,930 --> 00:57:15,432
I figure if I bury
this muzzle

856
00:57:15,515 --> 00:57:18,226
deep enough in his back,
nobody will hear the shot.

857
00:57:18,310 --> 00:57:20,687
Careful, Buford. You ain't
got but one bullet with that.

858
00:57:20,771 --> 00:57:22,564
I only need one.

859
00:57:34,326 --> 00:57:36,203
I told you to watch
your back, smithy.

860
00:57:36,286 --> 00:57:38,497
Tannen, you're early.

861
00:57:38,580 --> 00:57:40,958
It's a Derringer, smithy.
Small but effective.

862
00:57:41,041 --> 00:57:43,752
The last time I used it, fellow
took two whole days to die.

863
00:57:43,835 --> 00:57:44,836
Bled to death inside.

864
00:57:44,920 --> 00:57:46,630
It was real, real painful.

865
00:57:46,713 --> 00:57:50,342
That means you'll be dead
by about supper time Monday.

866
00:57:50,425 --> 00:57:53,804
Excuse me. I don't know who
you think you are, but we're dancing.

867
00:57:53,887 --> 00:57:56,014
Well, look at what
we have here.

868
00:57:56,098 --> 00:57:57,698
Ain't you gonna
introduce me to the lady?

869
00:57:57,724 --> 00:57:58,767
I'd like a dance.

870
00:57:58,850 --> 00:58:00,130
I wouldn't give
you the pleasure.

871
00:58:00,185 --> 00:58:02,604
You'll just have to
go ahead and shoot.

872
00:58:02,688 --> 00:58:03,689
All right.

873
00:58:03,772 --> 00:58:04,999
No, no. Emmett,
I'll dance with him.

874
00:58:05,023 --> 00:58:06,817
Boys, keep the
blacksmith company

875
00:58:06,900 --> 00:58:08,819
while I get acquainted
with the filly!

876
00:58:15,033 --> 00:58:18,453
I don't dance very well when
my partner has a gun in his hand.

877
00:58:18,537 --> 00:58:21,039
You'll learn.
You'll learn.

878
00:58:21,123 --> 00:58:24,376
You know, smithy, maybe I'll
just take my $80 worth out of her!

879
00:58:24,459 --> 00:58:25,669
Tannen,
leave her alone!

880
00:58:27,087 --> 00:58:28,797
Whoo!

881
00:58:29,423 --> 00:58:32,884
Yeah, I bet there's something
you can do that's worth $80.

882
00:58:34,177 --> 00:58:36,680
I believe you've
underestimated me, mister.

883
00:58:36,763 --> 00:58:38,223
Have I?

884
00:58:42,102 --> 00:58:43,353
Ow!

885
00:58:45,689 --> 00:58:48,692
Stop it!
Damn you, Tannen!

886
00:58:48,775 --> 00:58:51,194
No, I damn you!

887
00:58:52,696 --> 00:58:55,365
I damn you to hell!

888
00:59:01,997 --> 00:59:03,165
You.

889
00:59:03,248 --> 00:59:04,833
Hey, lighten up, jerk!

890
00:59:08,962 --> 00:59:10,922
Mighty strong words,
runt!

891
00:59:11,006 --> 00:59:12,466
You man enough
to back them up

892
00:59:12,549 --> 00:59:15,052
with more than
just a pie plate?

893
00:59:17,429 --> 00:59:20,557
Look, just leave
my friends alone.

894
00:59:20,640 --> 00:59:23,101
What's wrong, dude?
You yellow?

895
00:59:30,650 --> 00:59:33,528
That's what I thought.
A yellow belly.

896
00:59:39,659 --> 00:59:42,537
Nobody calls me yellow.

897
00:59:43,163 --> 00:59:46,458
Then let's
finish it right now.

898
00:59:46,541 --> 00:59:49,294
Not now, Buford.
Marshal's got our guns.

899
00:59:49,377 --> 00:59:51,505
Like I said,
we'll finish this tomorrow!

900
00:59:51,588 --> 00:59:54,883
Tomorrow we're robbing
the Pine City stage.

901
00:59:54,966 --> 00:59:57,427
What about Monday?
We doing anything Monday?

902
00:59:57,511 --> 00:59:59,530
No, Monday would be fine.
You can kill him on Monday.

903
00:59:59,554 --> 01:00:01,807
I'll be back
this way on Monday.

904
01:00:01,890 --> 01:00:03,475
We'll settle this then.

905
01:00:03,558 --> 01:00:06,269
Right there
out in the street

906
01:00:06,353 --> 01:00:07,687
in front of
the Palace Saloon.

907
01:00:09,314 --> 01:00:11,399
Yeah, right. When?

908
01:00:11,483 --> 01:00:13,360
High noon?
Noon?

909
01:00:13,860 --> 01:00:17,656
I do my killing
before breakfast. 7:00!

910
01:00:20,200 --> 01:00:24,913
8:00. I do my killing
after breakfast.

911
01:00:24,996 --> 01:00:26,373
Marty!

912
01:00:31,086 --> 01:00:32,166
All
right, now. Break it up.

913
01:00:32,212 --> 01:00:33,255
What's all this about?

914
01:00:33,338 --> 01:00:36,049
You causing
trouble here, Tannen?

915
01:00:36,133 --> 01:00:37,843
No trouble, Marshal.

916
01:00:37,926 --> 01:00:41,221
Just a little personal matter
between me and Eastwood.

917
01:00:41,930 --> 01:00:43,223
This don't
concern the law.

918
01:00:43,306 --> 01:00:46,184
Tonight, everything concerns
the law. Now break it up.

919
01:00:46,268 --> 01:00:49,688
Any brawling, it's 15
days in the county jail.

920
01:00:49,771 --> 01:00:51,982
All right, folks.
Come on, this is a party.

921
01:00:52,065 --> 01:00:54,025
Come on!
Let's have some fun!

922
01:00:59,281 --> 01:01:01,491
8:00 Monday, runt.

923
01:01:01,575 --> 01:01:02,993
If you ain't here,

924
01:01:03,076 --> 01:01:06,955
I'll hunt you and shoot
you down like a duck.

925
01:01:07,038 --> 01:01:10,208
It's "dog," Buford. Shoot
him down like a dog.

926
01:01:10,292 --> 01:01:14,921
Let's go, boys! Let these
sissies have their party!

927
01:01:16,173 --> 01:01:17,174
Marty,
what are you doing,

928
01:01:17,257 --> 01:01:18,633
saying you're
gonna meet Tannen?

929
01:01:18,717 --> 01:01:20,719
Hey, Doc, Doc,
don't worry about it.

930
01:01:20,802 --> 01:01:21,845
Monday morning 8:00 a.m.

931
01:01:21,928 --> 01:01:23,263
We're gonna
be gone, right?

932
01:01:23,346 --> 01:01:26,308
Theoretically, yes, but
what if the train's late?

933
01:01:26,391 --> 01:01:27,809
Late? We'll discuss this later.

934
01:01:27,893 --> 01:01:29,120
No, we'll discuss it now.
Late? What do you mean?

935
01:01:29,144 --> 01:01:30,413
Thank you for your
gallantry, Mr. Eastwood.

936
01:01:30,437 --> 01:01:31,688
Hey, ma'am.

937
01:01:31,771 --> 01:01:34,149
Had you not interceded,
Emmett might have been shot.

938
01:01:34,232 --> 01:01:36,985
Marty... Clint, I'm
gonna take Clara home.

939
01:01:37,068 --> 01:01:38,462
Right. Good night,
Doc. Good night, Clara.

940
01:01:38,486 --> 01:01:40,197
You sure set him straight,
Mr. Eastwood.

941
01:01:40,280 --> 01:01:41,424
I'm glad somebody
finally got the gumption

942
01:01:41,448 --> 01:01:43,116
to stand up to
that son of a bitch.

943
01:01:43,200 --> 01:01:44,719
You're all right in
my book, Mr. Eastwood.

944
01:01:44,743 --> 01:01:45,952
I'd like to
buy you a drink.

945
01:01:46,036 --> 01:01:47,096
Hey, listen,
I don't want a drink.

946
01:01:47,120 --> 01:01:48,890
You don't have to buy me
anything. Young man. Young man.

947
01:01:48,914 --> 01:01:50,933
It was no big deal. I'd like
for you to have this brand-new

948
01:01:50,957 --> 01:01:54,002
Colt Peacemaker and
gun belt free of charge.

949
01:01:55,295 --> 01:01:56,379
Free?

950
01:01:56,463 --> 01:01:57,881
I want everybody
to know

951
01:01:57,964 --> 01:02:01,760
that the gun that shot Buford
Tannen was a Colt Peacemaker!

952
01:02:01,843 --> 01:02:04,638
Hey, no problem.
Thanks a lot.

953
01:02:04,721 --> 01:02:06,139
Of course
you understand

954
01:02:06,223 --> 01:02:08,600
that if you lose,
I'm taking it back.

955
01:02:11,811 --> 01:02:13,313
Thanks again.

956
01:02:16,608 --> 01:02:18,235
You had him,
Mr. Eastwood.

957
01:02:18,318 --> 01:02:19,462
You could have
just walked away,

958
01:02:19,486 --> 01:02:21,738
and nobody would have
thought the less of you for it.

959
01:02:21,821 --> 01:02:26,034
All it would have been was
words, hot air from a buffoon.

960
01:02:26,117 --> 01:02:27,786
Instead,
you let him rile you.

961
01:02:27,869 --> 01:02:31,915
Rile you into playing his
game, his way, by his rules.

962
01:02:31,998 --> 01:02:34,376
Seamus, relax.
I know what I'm doing.

963
01:02:34,459 --> 01:02:35,519
He reminds me
of poor Martin.

964
01:02:35,543 --> 01:02:36,544
Aye.

965
01:02:36,628 --> 01:02:37,796
Who?
Me brother.

966
01:02:37,879 --> 01:02:38,981
Wait a minute.
Wait a minute.

967
01:02:39,005 --> 01:02:41,049
You have a brother
named Martin McFly?

968
01:02:41,132 --> 01:02:43,551
Had a brother.

969
01:02:43,635 --> 01:02:46,304
Martin used to let men
provoke him into fighting.

970
01:02:46,388 --> 01:02:49,266
He was concerned that people
would think him a coward if he refused.

971
01:02:49,349 --> 01:02:51,726
That's how he got a Bowie
knife shoved through his belly

972
01:02:51,810 --> 01:02:54,688
in a saloon
in Virginia City.

973
01:02:54,771 --> 01:02:56,606
Never considered
the future, poor Martin.

974
01:02:56,690 --> 01:02:58,608
God rest his soul.

975
01:03:00,110 --> 01:03:03,947
Sure, and I hope you're
considering the future, Mr. Eastwood.

976
01:03:07,200 --> 01:03:09,703
I think about it
all the time.

977
01:03:10,578 --> 01:03:12,289
And that crater
in the middle north west,

978
01:03:12,372 --> 01:03:14,350
the one that's out there
all by itself like a star burst...

979
01:03:14,374 --> 01:03:15,417
Uh-huh.

980
01:03:15,500 --> 01:03:17,752
...that one's
called Copernicus.

981
01:03:18,753 --> 01:03:21,214
Listen to me. I feel
like I'm teaching school.

982
01:03:22,382 --> 01:03:24,884
Please,
continue the lesson.

983
01:03:24,968 --> 01:03:27,971
I never found lunar
geography so fascinating.

984
01:03:28,054 --> 01:03:30,140
You're quite
knowledgeable.

985
01:03:30,223 --> 01:03:31,975
When I was 11,
I had diphtheria.

986
01:03:32,058 --> 01:03:34,060
I was quarantined
for three months.

987
01:03:34,144 --> 01:03:36,229
So my father
brought this telescope,

988
01:03:36,313 --> 01:03:37,689
and he put it
next to my bed

989
01:03:37,772 --> 01:03:40,317
so I could see
everything out the window.

990
01:03:41,484 --> 01:03:44,821
Emmett, do you think we'll
ever be able to travel to the moon

991
01:03:44,904 --> 01:03:47,991
the way we travel across
the country on trains?

992
01:03:48,074 --> 01:03:51,494
Definitely. Although not for
84 years and not on trains.

993
01:03:51,578 --> 01:03:55,415
We'll have space vehicles,
capsules sent aloft with rockets.

994
01:03:55,498 --> 01:03:57,334
Devices that
create giant explosions.

995
01:03:57,417 --> 01:03:59,836
Explosions so powerful
that they just...

996
01:03:59,919 --> 01:04:02,005
That they break the pull
of the Earth's gravity

997
01:04:02,088 --> 01:04:05,342
and send the projectile
through outer space.

998
01:04:06,760 --> 01:04:09,137
Emmett,
I read that book, too!

999
01:04:09,220 --> 01:04:11,222
You're quoting
Jules Verne

1000
01:04:11,306 --> 01:04:12,766
From the Earth
to the Moon.

1001
01:04:12,849 --> 01:04:15,101
You've read Jules Verne?

1002
01:04:15,894 --> 01:04:17,812
I adore Jules Verne.

1003
01:04:17,896 --> 01:04:19,522
So do I.

1004
01:04:19,606 --> 01:04:22,484
Twenty Thousand Leagues
Under the Sea. My absolute favorite.

1005
01:04:22,567 --> 01:04:24,545
The first time I read that
when I was a little boy,

1006
01:04:24,569 --> 01:04:27,155
why, I wanted to
meet Captain Nemo.

1007
01:04:27,238 --> 01:04:28,406
Don't tease, Emmett.

1008
01:04:28,490 --> 01:04:30,370
You couldn't have read
that when you were a boy.

1009
01:04:30,450 --> 01:04:32,869
It was only first
published 10 years ago.

1010
01:04:33,119 --> 01:04:36,998
Oh, yes. Well, I meant it
made me feel like a boy.

1011
01:04:39,292 --> 01:04:43,922
I never met a woman who
liked Jules Verne before.

1012
01:04:45,965 --> 01:04:49,386
I never ever met a man
like you before.

1013
01:05:41,771 --> 01:05:43,189
Doc?

1014
01:05:47,652 --> 01:05:48,778
Doc?

1015
01:05:51,781 --> 01:05:54,159
Hope you know
what you're doing.

1016
01:06:07,380 --> 01:06:09,215
You talking to me?

1017
01:06:11,885 --> 01:06:14,220
You talking to me,
Tannen?

1018
01:06:18,057 --> 01:06:20,643
Well, I'm the
only one here.

1019
01:06:20,727 --> 01:06:24,397
Go ahead,
make my day.

1020
01:06:30,904 --> 01:06:32,405
Say good morning,
Mr. Eastwood.

1021
01:06:32,489 --> 01:06:33,781
Morning.

1022
01:06:33,865 --> 01:06:35,492
Have a cigar,
Mr. Eastwood.

1023
01:06:35,575 --> 01:06:38,077
Anything I can do for
you today, Mr. Eastwood?

1024
01:06:38,161 --> 01:06:39,704
No, that's fine.

1025
01:06:39,787 --> 01:06:41,331
Good luck tomorrow,
Mr. Eastwood.

1026
01:06:41,414 --> 01:06:42,999
We'll be praying
for you.

1027
01:06:43,082 --> 01:06:44,125
Thanks.

1028
01:06:45,418 --> 01:06:47,504
Good morning,
Mr. Eastwood.

1029
01:06:47,587 --> 01:06:49,589
Interest you in a new
suit for tomorrow?

1030
01:06:51,633 --> 01:06:53,635
I'm fine. Thanks.

1031
01:07:04,771 --> 01:07:06,856
Doc, what are
you doing?

1032
01:07:06,940 --> 01:07:08,316
Oh, nothing.

1033
01:07:08,399 --> 01:07:10,985
I was just out
enjoying the morning air.

1034
01:07:11,069 --> 01:07:13,404
It's really lovely here in the
morning. Don't you think?

1035
01:07:13,488 --> 01:07:14,531
Yeah, it's lovely, Doc.

1036
01:07:14,614 --> 01:07:15,925
So listen, we've got
to load the Delorean up

1037
01:07:15,949 --> 01:07:17,534
and get ready
to roll, all right?

1038
01:07:17,617 --> 01:07:20,119
Hey, look at that,
the tombstone.

1039
01:07:21,829 --> 01:07:24,707
Marty, let me see
that photograph again.

1040
01:07:26,501 --> 01:07:29,295
My name,
it's vanished.

1041
01:07:29,379 --> 01:07:31,756
Hey, that's great, Doc.
Don't you get it?

1042
01:07:31,839 --> 01:07:32,983
We're going back to
the future tomorrow,

1043
01:07:33,007 --> 01:07:34,342
so everything's
being erased.

1044
01:07:34,425 --> 01:07:36,219
But only my name
Is erased.

1045
01:07:36,302 --> 01:07:38,113
The tombstone itself
and the date still remain.

1046
01:07:38,137 --> 01:07:40,056
That doesn't
make sense.

1047
01:07:40,139 --> 01:07:42,725
We know that this photograph
represents what will happen

1048
01:07:42,809 --> 01:07:44,227
if the events
of today continue

1049
01:07:44,310 --> 01:07:46,145
to run their course
into tomorrow.

1050
01:07:46,229 --> 01:07:47,230
Right. So?

1051
01:07:47,313 --> 01:07:48,565
Excuse me,
Mr. Eastwood.

1052
01:07:48,648 --> 01:07:50,125
I just need to take
your measurements.

1053
01:07:50,149 --> 01:07:52,193
Oh, look, pal, I don't
want to buy a suit.

1054
01:07:53,361 --> 01:07:55,488
No, this is
for your coffin.

1055
01:07:55,572 --> 01:07:56,614
My coffin?

1056
01:07:56,698 --> 01:07:59,158
Well, the odds are running
two to one against you.

1057
01:07:59,242 --> 01:08:01,578
Might as well
be prepared.

1058
01:08:01,661 --> 01:08:05,123
So it may not be my name that's
supposed to end up on this tombstone.

1059
01:08:05,206 --> 01:08:06,958
It may be yours.

1060
01:08:08,209 --> 01:08:09,252
Great Scott.

1061
01:08:09,335 --> 01:08:10,335
I know.
This is heavy.

1062
01:08:10,378 --> 01:08:12,338
Marty, why are you
wearing that gun?

1063
01:08:12,422 --> 01:08:14,567
You're not considering going
up against Tannen tomorrow?

1064
01:08:14,591 --> 01:08:17,427
Doc, tomorrow morning I'm
going back to the future with you.

1065
01:08:17,510 --> 01:08:19,321
But if Buford Tannen
comes looking for trouble,

1066
01:08:19,345 --> 01:08:20,930
I'm gonna be
ready for him.

1067
01:08:21,014 --> 01:08:23,391
You heard what that son of
a bitch called me last night.

1068
01:08:23,474 --> 01:08:24,785
Marty, you can't go
losing your judgment

1069
01:08:24,809 --> 01:08:26,269
every time someone
calls you a name.

1070
01:08:26,352 --> 01:08:27,413
That's exactly
what causes you

1071
01:08:27,437 --> 01:08:30,690
to get into that accident
in the future.

1072
01:08:32,025 --> 01:08:35,778
What?
What about my future?

1073
01:08:35,862 --> 01:08:38,990
I can't tell you. It might
make things worse.

1074
01:08:39,073 --> 01:08:42,869
Wait a minute. Doc, what
is wrong with my future?

1075
01:08:43,786 --> 01:08:45,997
Marty, we all have
to make decisions

1076
01:08:46,080 --> 01:08:48,458
that affect
the course of our lives.

1077
01:08:48,541 --> 01:08:51,461
You've got to do
what you've got to do,

1078
01:08:51,544 --> 01:08:53,379
and I've got to do
what I've got to do.

1079
01:09:04,891 --> 01:09:06,934
Marty.
Yeah.

1080
01:09:07,018 --> 01:09:08,811
I've made a decision.
Uh-huh.

1081
01:09:08,895 --> 01:09:10,135
I'm not going
with you tomorrow.

1082
01:09:11,105 --> 01:09:12,815
I'm staying here.

1083
01:09:16,194 --> 01:09:19,030
What are you
talking about, Doc?

1084
01:09:19,113 --> 01:09:20,990
There's no point
in denying it.

1085
01:09:21,074 --> 01:09:22,158
I'm in love with Clara.

1086
01:09:22,241 --> 01:09:23,618
Oh, man.

1087
01:09:23,701 --> 01:09:27,789
Doc, we don't belong
here. Neither one of us.

1088
01:09:27,872 --> 01:09:30,750
You know, it could still be
you that gets shot tomorrow.

1089
01:09:30,833 --> 01:09:33,378
This tombstone could
still be in your future.

1090
01:09:33,461 --> 01:09:36,798
Marty, the future
isn't written.

1091
01:09:36,881 --> 01:09:39,300
It can be changed.
You know that.

1092
01:09:40,718 --> 01:09:44,389
Anyone can make their future
whatever they want it to be.

1093
01:09:44,472 --> 01:09:46,182
I can't let this
one little photograph

1094
01:09:46,265 --> 01:09:48,851
determine
my entire destiny.

1095
01:09:48,935 --> 01:09:50,186
I have to
live my life

1096
01:09:50,269 --> 01:09:54,107
according to what I
believe is right in my heart.

1097
01:09:56,442 --> 01:09:59,320
Doc, you're a scientist.

1098
01:10:01,280 --> 01:10:05,785
So you tell me. What's the
right thing to do up here?

1099
01:10:19,632 --> 01:10:21,008
You're right, Marty.

1100
01:10:43,906 --> 01:10:45,074
Wow, that worked great.

1101
01:10:45,158 --> 01:10:48,077
I've at least got
to tell her goodbye.

1102
01:10:48,995 --> 01:10:51,956
Come on, Doc.
Think about it.

1103
01:10:52,039 --> 01:10:53,416
What are you
gonna say to her?

1104
01:10:53,499 --> 01:10:54,917
"I've got to go
back to the future?"

1105
01:10:55,001 --> 01:10:57,253
I mean, she's not gonna
understand that, Doc.

1106
01:10:57,336 --> 01:10:58,379
Hell, I'm in it
with you,

1107
01:10:58,463 --> 01:11:01,007
and I don't
even understand it.

1108
01:11:03,593 --> 01:11:06,554
Doc, listen.

1109
01:11:07,847 --> 01:11:09,432
Maybe we...

1110
01:11:09,515 --> 01:11:12,894
I don't know, maybe we
can just take Clara with us.

1111
01:11:13,978 --> 01:11:15,521
To the future?

1112
01:11:19,776 --> 01:11:22,445
As you reminded me,
Marty, I'm a scientist,

1113
01:11:22,528 --> 01:11:25,198
so I must be
scientific about this.

1114
01:11:25,281 --> 01:11:27,116
I cautioned you about
disrupting the continuum

1115
01:11:27,200 --> 01:11:28,534
for your own
personal benefit.

1116
01:11:28,618 --> 01:11:30,870
Therefore,
I must do no less.

1117
01:11:30,953 --> 01:11:32,079
We shall proceed
as planned,

1118
01:11:32,163 --> 01:11:34,123
and as soon as
we return to 1985,

1119
01:11:34,207 --> 01:11:37,710
we'll destroy this
infernal machine.

1120
01:11:37,794 --> 01:11:41,255
Traveling through time has
become much too painful.

1121
01:12:11,702 --> 01:12:13,496
It's Emmett, Clara.

1122
01:12:17,583 --> 01:12:20,336
Emmett,
won't you come in?

1123
01:12:20,419 --> 01:12:23,548
No, I better not. I...

1124
01:12:25,800 --> 01:12:27,093
What's wrong?

1125
01:12:28,594 --> 01:12:30,137
I've come
to say goodbye.

1126
01:12:31,931 --> 01:12:34,433
Goodbye? Well,
where are you going??

1127
01:12:35,101 --> 01:12:37,019
I'm going away.

1128
01:12:37,103 --> 01:12:39,772
And well, I'm afraid
I'll never see you again.

1129
01:12:41,274 --> 01:12:42,608
Emmett!

1130
01:12:42,692 --> 01:12:47,905
Clara, I want you to know
that I care about you deeply.

1131
01:12:48,948 --> 01:12:50,628
But I've realized that
I don't belong here,

1132
01:12:50,700 --> 01:12:54,370
and I have to go back
where I came from.

1133
01:12:54,453 --> 01:12:56,998
And where
might that be?

1134
01:12:57,081 --> 01:12:58,791
I can't tell you.

1135
01:13:01,085 --> 01:13:04,881
Well, wherever you're
going, take me with you.

1136
01:13:06,215 --> 01:13:08,467
I can't, Clara.

1137
01:13:08,551 --> 01:13:09,945
I wish it didn't
have to be this way,

1138
01:13:09,969 --> 01:13:11,512
but just believe me
when I tell you

1139
01:13:11,596 --> 01:13:16,017
that I'll never forget you
and that I love you.

1140
01:13:18,185 --> 01:13:20,438
I don't understand
what you're trying to say.

1141
01:13:20,521 --> 01:13:24,442
Clara, I don't think there's any
way that you can understand it.

1142
01:13:25,151 --> 01:13:27,528
Please, Emmett.
Please, I have to know.

1143
01:13:27,612 --> 01:13:29,238
If you sincerely
do love me,

1144
01:13:29,322 --> 01:13:30,656
then tell me
the truth.

1145
01:13:32,742 --> 01:13:34,410
All right then.

1146
01:13:35,453 --> 01:13:36,662
I'm from the future.

1147
01:13:38,706 --> 01:13:41,709
I came here in a time
machine that I invented,

1148
01:13:41,792 --> 01:13:45,838
and tomorrow I have to
go back to the year 1985.

1149
01:13:52,094 --> 01:13:53,512
Yes, Emmett.

1150
01:13:54,931 --> 01:13:56,599
I do understand.

1151
01:13:59,185 --> 01:14:01,520
I understand that
because you know

1152
01:14:01,604 --> 01:14:03,898
I'm partial to the
writings of Jules Verne,

1153
01:14:03,981 --> 01:14:05,399
you concocted those mendacities

1154
01:14:05,483 --> 01:14:07,526
in order to take
advantage of me.

1155
01:14:08,486 --> 01:14:10,947
Oh! I've heard some
whoppers in my day,

1156
01:14:11,030 --> 01:14:12,215
but the fact
that you'd expect me

1157
01:14:12,239 --> 01:14:13,532
to entertain
a notion like that

1158
01:14:13,616 --> 01:14:16,369
is so insulting
and degrading!

1159
01:14:18,162 --> 01:14:19,956
All you had to say is,
"I don't love you,

1160
01:14:20,039 --> 01:14:22,124
"and I don't want
to see you anymore."

1161
01:14:22,208 --> 01:14:24,669
That, at least, would
have been respectful!

1162
01:14:25,419 --> 01:14:26,754
But that's
not the truth.

1163
01:15:06,419 --> 01:15:08,295
Good to see you, too.

1164
01:15:09,588 --> 01:15:13,134
Emmett. What can I
get you? The usual?

1165
01:15:13,217 --> 01:15:14,802
No, Chester, I'm
gonna need something

1166
01:15:14,885 --> 01:15:17,013
a lot stronger
than that tonight.

1167
01:15:17,096 --> 01:15:18,514
Sarsaparilla.

1168
01:15:18,597 --> 01:15:19,974
Whiskey, Chester.

1169
01:15:20,850 --> 01:15:23,269
Whiskey?
Emmett, are you sure?

1170
01:15:23,352 --> 01:15:25,688
You know what happened
to you on the Fourth of July.

1171
01:15:25,771 --> 01:15:26,981
Whiskey.

1172
01:15:28,357 --> 01:15:31,569
Okay, I ain't your papa.

1173
01:15:31,652 --> 01:15:34,905
Just don't want to see
you do the wrong thing.

1174
01:15:36,240 --> 01:15:38,284
You can leave
the bottle.

1175
01:15:40,119 --> 01:15:41,954
It's a woman, right?

1176
01:15:43,622 --> 01:15:45,875
I knew it. I have seen
that look on a man's face

1177
01:15:45,958 --> 01:15:48,836
a thousand times
all across the country.

1178
01:15:48,919 --> 01:15:51,255
All I can tell you, friend,
is you'll get over her.

1179
01:15:51,338 --> 01:15:54,800
Oh... Clara was
one in a million.

1180
01:15:55,843 --> 01:16:00,097
One in a billion.
One in a googolplex.

1181
01:16:00,181 --> 01:16:03,809
The woman of my dreams,
and I've lost her for all time.

1182
01:16:05,102 --> 01:16:08,564
I can assure you, Sir,
there are other women.

1183
01:16:08,647 --> 01:16:11,275
Why, peddling this barbed
wire all across the country

1184
01:16:11,358 --> 01:16:13,694
has taught me
one thing for certain,

1185
01:16:13,778 --> 01:16:16,739
is that you never know
what the future might bring.

1186
01:16:16,822 --> 01:16:20,701
The future. Oh, I can
tell you about the future.

1187
01:16:31,170 --> 01:16:33,130
Oh, man, did I sleep.

1188
01:16:36,175 --> 01:16:38,010
What time is it, Doc?

1189
01:16:42,014 --> 01:16:43,182
Doc?

1190
01:16:54,527 --> 01:16:55,778
Wake up!

1191
01:16:57,530 --> 01:16:59,448
Get up! Let's go!

1192
01:17:00,699 --> 01:17:02,701
I got me
a runt to Kill.

1193
01:17:02,785 --> 01:17:05,746
It's still early, boss.
What's your hurry?

1194
01:17:06,789 --> 01:17:08,082
I'm hungry.

1195
01:17:08,874 --> 01:17:11,919
And in the future,
we don't need horses.

1196
01:17:13,629 --> 01:17:17,049
We have motorized
carriages called automobiles.

1197
01:17:18,217 --> 01:17:21,595
If everybody's got one
of these auto whatsits,

1198
01:17:21,679 --> 01:17:24,098
does anybody walk
or run anymore?

1199
01:17:24,765 --> 01:17:28,686
Of course we run, but
for recreation, for fun.

1200
01:17:28,769 --> 01:17:32,439
Run for fun? What the
hell kind of fun is that?

1201
01:17:42,700 --> 01:17:44,910
Doc! Doc!

1202
01:17:51,750 --> 01:17:53,544
How much
has he had?

1203
01:17:53,627 --> 01:17:55,337
None.
That's the first one,

1204
01:17:55,421 --> 01:17:56,922
and he hasn't
touched it yet.

1205
01:17:57,756 --> 01:17:59,341
He just likes
to hold it.

1206
01:18:02,887 --> 01:18:05,848
Doc! Doc!
What are you doing?

1207
01:18:06,849 --> 01:18:10,144
I've lost her, Marty. There's
nothing left for me here.

1208
01:18:10,227 --> 01:18:12,187
All right, that's why you
gotta come back with me.

1209
01:18:12,229 --> 01:18:13,229
Where?

1210
01:18:13,272 --> 01:18:14,481
Back to the future!

1211
01:18:18,777 --> 01:18:20,696
Right!
Let's get going!

1212
01:18:20,779 --> 01:18:21,864
Great.

1213
01:18:21,947 --> 01:18:25,117
Gentlemen, excuse me, but my
friend and I have to catch a train.

1214
01:18:25,201 --> 01:18:26,911
Here's to you,
blacksmith.

1215
01:18:26,994 --> 01:18:28,287
And to the future.

1216
01:18:28,370 --> 01:18:29,413
Amen.

1217
01:18:29,872 --> 01:18:31,457
Amen.
Emmett, no!

1218
01:18:39,632 --> 01:18:42,593
Doc! Doc! Doc!

1219
01:18:43,510 --> 01:18:46,555
Come on, Doc.
Wake up. Wake up, Doc!

1220
01:18:47,598 --> 01:18:48,598
How many did he have?

1221
01:18:48,641 --> 01:18:49,767
Just the one.

1222
01:18:49,850 --> 01:18:51,977
Just the one?
Come on, Doc.

1223
01:18:52,061 --> 01:18:54,313
There's a fellow that
can't hold his liquor.

1224
01:18:54,396 --> 01:18:56,065
Get me some coffee.
Black!

1225
01:18:56,148 --> 01:18:57,274
Joey, coffee!

1226
01:19:07,284 --> 01:19:09,411
Ma'am?

1227
01:19:09,495 --> 01:19:11,413
How far does
the 8:00 train go?

1228
01:19:11,497 --> 01:19:13,832
San Francisco is
the end of the line.

1229
01:19:15,501 --> 01:19:17,670
I'll take
a one-way ticket.

1230
01:19:19,004 --> 01:19:21,048
You want to sober
him up in a hurry, son,

1231
01:19:21,131 --> 01:19:23,151
you're gonna have to use
something a lot stronger than coffee.

1232
01:19:23,175 --> 01:19:25,803
Yeah,
what do you suggest?

1233
01:19:25,886 --> 01:19:29,265
Joey, let's make
some wake-up juice.

1234
01:19:29,348 --> 01:19:31,850
Come on, Doc, swallow.
Here we go.

1235
01:19:36,939 --> 01:19:38,941
In about 10 minutes,

1236
01:19:39,024 --> 01:19:42,027
he's gonna be as sober
as a priest on Sunday.

1237
01:19:43,070 --> 01:19:44,238
Ten minutes!

1238
01:19:44,321 --> 01:19:46,782
Why do we have to cut
these things so damn close?

1239
01:19:46,865 --> 01:19:49,576
Here, stick this
clothespin on his nose.

1240
01:19:51,453 --> 01:19:53,539
When he opens up
his mouth,

1241
01:19:53,622 --> 01:19:57,084
go ahead and pour
it on down his gullet.

1242
01:19:57,167 --> 01:19:58,877
Oh, and stand back.

1243
01:20:17,354 --> 01:20:18,355
He's still out!

1244
01:20:18,439 --> 01:20:20,649
Oh, that? That was
just a reflex action.

1245
01:20:20,733 --> 01:20:22,151
It's gonna take
a few more minutes

1246
01:20:22,234 --> 01:20:24,069
for the stuff to really
clear up his head.

1247
01:20:24,153 --> 01:20:25,362
Perfect.

1248
01:20:46,633 --> 01:20:48,761
Come on, Doc.
Come on, wake up, buddy.

1249
01:20:48,844 --> 01:20:51,722
Wake up, Doc!
Come on. Come on.

1250
01:20:51,805 --> 01:20:54,391
Seamus, wouldn't expect
to see you here this morning.

1251
01:20:54,475 --> 01:20:58,437
Aye. Something inside
me told me I should be here

1252
01:20:59,438 --> 01:21:02,608
as if my future had
something to do with it.

1253
01:21:04,276 --> 01:21:06,737
He'll come around
in a minute. Come on.

1254
01:21:06,820 --> 01:21:08,781
Come on, Doc.
Let's go, Doc!

1255
01:21:08,864 --> 01:21:10,783
Let's wake up now, buddy.
Come on.

1256
01:21:17,831 --> 01:21:20,125
Are you in there,
Eastwood?

1257
01:21:22,086 --> 01:21:25,130
It's 8:00,
and I'm calling you out.

1258
01:21:30,094 --> 01:21:31,762
It's not 8:00 yet!

1259
01:21:32,179 --> 01:21:34,139
It is by my watch!

1260
01:21:34,223 --> 01:21:37,142
Let's settle this
once and for all, runt!

1261
01:21:38,811 --> 01:21:41,105
Or ain't you
got the gumption?

1262
01:21:50,572 --> 01:21:51,824
Listen.

1263
01:21:54,326 --> 01:21:57,162
I'm not really feeling
up to this today.

1264
01:21:58,747 --> 01:22:00,374
So I'm gonna
have to forfeit.

1265
01:22:01,041 --> 01:22:03,335
Forfeit?

1266
01:22:03,961 --> 01:22:05,712
What's that mean?

1267
01:22:07,089 --> 01:22:09,091
It means that you win
without a fight.

1268
01:22:09,174 --> 01:22:10,968
Without shooting?
He can't do that.

1269
01:22:11,051 --> 01:22:13,178
Hey, you can't do that!

1270
01:22:13,262 --> 01:22:14,763
You know what I think?

1271
01:22:14,847 --> 01:22:18,183
I think you ain't nothing
but a gutless, yellow turd!

1272
01:22:18,267 --> 01:22:20,727
And I'm giving you
to the count of 10

1273
01:22:20,811 --> 01:22:22,938
to come out here
and prove I'm wrong!

1274
01:22:23,856 --> 01:22:25,149
One!

1275
01:22:25,232 --> 01:22:27,818
Doc, come on, sober up,
buddy. Let's go. Come on.

1276
01:22:27,901 --> 01:22:28,944
Two!

1277
01:22:29,027 --> 01:22:30,279
You better get
out there, son.

1278
01:22:30,362 --> 01:22:33,490
I got $20 gold bet on you,
so don't let me down.

1279
01:22:33,574 --> 01:22:34,783
Three!

1280
01:22:34,867 --> 01:22:39,079
I got $30 gold bet against
you, so don't let me down.

1281
01:22:39,163 --> 01:22:40,164
Four!

1282
01:22:40,247 --> 01:22:42,207
You better face
up to it, son,

1283
01:22:42,291 --> 01:22:44,710
because if you
don't go out there...

1284
01:22:44,793 --> 01:22:46,253
What?
Five!

1285
01:22:47,754 --> 01:22:49,339
What if I don't
go out there?

1286
01:22:49,423 --> 01:22:50,674
You're a coward.

1287
01:22:50,757 --> 01:22:51,842
Six!

1288
01:22:51,925 --> 01:22:55,095
And you'll be branded a
coward for the rest of your days!

1289
01:22:55,179 --> 01:22:59,308
Everybody everywhere
will say Clint Eastwood

1290
01:22:59,391 --> 01:23:02,102
is the biggest yellow
belly in the West!

1291
01:23:07,232 --> 01:23:08,275
Seven!

1292
01:23:09,318 --> 01:23:10,444
Here!

1293
01:23:14,573 --> 01:23:15,741
Eight!

1294
01:23:24,583 --> 01:23:25,792
I already got a gun.

1295
01:23:27,878 --> 01:23:29,129
Nine!

1296
01:23:33,967 --> 01:23:35,010
Ten!

1297
01:23:40,098 --> 01:23:41,808
Do you hear me, runt?

1298
01:23:41,892 --> 01:23:46,104
I said that's 10, you
gutless, yellow pie-stinger!

1299
01:23:49,775 --> 01:23:52,027
He's an asshole!

1300
01:23:52,110 --> 01:23:54,071
I don't care
what Tannen says!

1301
01:23:54,154 --> 01:23:57,115
And I don't care what
anybody else says either.

1302
01:23:58,242 --> 01:23:59,576
Doc! Doc, you okay?

1303
01:23:59,660 --> 01:24:00,702
I think so.

1304
01:24:01,954 --> 01:24:03,413
What a headache!

1305
01:24:03,497 --> 01:24:05,249
Listen, you got a
back door to this place?

1306
01:24:05,332 --> 01:24:06,375
Yeah, it's in the back.

1307
01:24:06,458 --> 01:24:07,501
Let's go, Doc.

1308
01:24:07,584 --> 01:24:11,129
Are you coming out here, or
do I have to come in after you?

1309
01:24:13,048 --> 01:24:14,967
The thing I really
miss here is Tylenol.

1310
01:24:15,842 --> 01:24:16,969
Hey!

1311
01:24:22,975 --> 01:24:24,518
Reach, blacksmith!

1312
01:24:41,493 --> 01:24:44,204
Yes, sir, that
poor fellow last night

1313
01:24:44,288 --> 01:24:48,250
had the worst case of
broken heart I have ever seen.

1314
01:24:48,333 --> 01:24:49,585
When he said
he didn't know

1315
01:24:49,668 --> 01:24:51,378
how he could live
the rest of his life

1316
01:24:51,461 --> 01:24:54,256
knowing how much hurt
he'd caused that little girl.

1317
01:24:54,339 --> 01:24:58,385
Well, I really felt for
him. I did. Right here.

1318
01:25:03,181 --> 01:25:04,933
Listen up, Eastwood!

1319
01:25:05,017 --> 01:25:06,560
I aim to shoot
somebody today,

1320
01:25:06,643 --> 01:25:08,437
and I prefer
it'd be you.

1321
01:25:08,520 --> 01:25:11,064
But if you're
just too damn yellow,

1322
01:25:11,148 --> 01:25:13,609
I guess it'll just have to
be your blacksmith friend.

1323
01:25:13,692 --> 01:25:16,111
Forget about me, Marty,
and save yourself!

1324
01:25:21,158 --> 01:25:23,285
You got one minute
to decide.

1325
01:25:23,368 --> 01:25:25,704
You hear me, runt?
One minute!

1326
01:25:28,915 --> 01:25:31,918
I've never seen a man so
broken up over a woman.

1327
01:25:32,002 --> 01:25:34,630
What did he say her
name was? Cara? Sara?

1328
01:25:34,713 --> 01:25:36,256
Clara.
Clara.

1329
01:25:39,551 --> 01:25:41,303
Excuse me.
Ma'am?

1330
01:25:42,471 --> 01:25:45,807
But was this man tall with
great big, brown puppy-dog eyes

1331
01:25:45,891 --> 01:25:48,393
and long silvery,
flowing hair?

1332
01:25:48,477 --> 01:25:49,686
You know him.

1333
01:25:52,564 --> 01:25:53,649
Emmett!

1334
01:26:08,497 --> 01:26:10,374
Time's up, runt!

1335
01:26:16,463 --> 01:26:19,299
Prepare to meet
your maker, blacksmith.

1336
01:26:19,383 --> 01:26:21,176
Right here, Tannen!

1337
01:26:58,964 --> 01:27:00,006
Draw!

1338
01:27:03,343 --> 01:27:04,469
No!

1339
01:27:20,068 --> 01:27:22,612
I thought we could
settle this like men.

1340
01:27:24,156 --> 01:27:26,158
You thought wrong, dude.

1341
01:27:48,346 --> 01:27:49,806
Thank you.

1342
01:28:53,370 --> 01:28:55,121
That was good.

1343
01:28:57,916 --> 01:28:59,876
You know what I think? No.

1344
01:28:59,960 --> 01:29:01,169
I think Buford's
going to jail.

1345
01:29:01,253 --> 01:29:02,379
Yeah.

1346
01:29:08,051 --> 01:29:10,136
Get him out
of that shit.

1347
01:29:10,220 --> 01:29:11,763
Get them!

1348
01:29:15,767 --> 01:29:18,353
Buford Tannen,
you're under arrest

1349
01:29:18,436 --> 01:29:20,397
for robbing
the Pine City stage.

1350
01:29:20,480 --> 01:29:22,607
You got anything to say?

1351
01:29:27,779 --> 01:29:29,573
I hate manure.

1352
01:29:29,656 --> 01:29:30,699
Look!

1353
01:29:34,452 --> 01:29:35,704
Yes!

1354
01:29:37,163 --> 01:29:38,164
The train!

1355
01:29:38,248 --> 01:29:39,457
Can we make it?

1356
01:29:39,541 --> 01:29:41,459
We'll have to cut them
off at Coyote Pass.

1357
01:29:43,962 --> 01:29:46,756
Hey! Hey, mister.
Mr. Eastwood.

1358
01:29:46,840 --> 01:29:48,049
Here's your gun, mister.

1359
01:29:48,133 --> 01:29:49,301
Thanks, kid.

1360
01:29:54,639 --> 01:29:56,349
Seamus!

1361
01:29:57,475 --> 01:29:59,436
It's worth $12.
Never been used.

1362
01:30:00,854 --> 01:30:02,939
Maybe I'll trade it
for a new hat.

1363
01:30:03,023 --> 01:30:05,358
Right, and take
care of that baby!

1364
01:30:06,568 --> 01:30:07,819
I willi

1365
01:30:14,492 --> 01:30:15,785
Emmett!

1366
01:30:18,163 --> 01:30:19,414
Emmett!

1367
01:30:20,874 --> 01:30:22,125
Emmett!

1368
01:30:27,005 --> 01:30:28,673
"Time machine."

1369
01:31:12,133 --> 01:31:13,134
Come on, Marty!

1370
01:31:16,888 --> 01:31:17,889
Go, Doc.

1371
01:31:17,973 --> 01:31:19,724
Come on.
Give me your hand!

1372
01:31:55,218 --> 01:31:56,636
Come on, Marty.

1373
01:32:01,141 --> 01:32:02,600
Come on. Let's go!

1374
01:32:11,276 --> 01:32:12,610
Masks on.

1375
01:32:27,792 --> 01:32:29,002
Reach!

1376
01:32:29,085 --> 01:32:30,253
Is this a holdup?

1377
01:32:32,255 --> 01:32:34,674
It's a science experiment.

1378
01:32:34,758 --> 01:32:37,594
Stop the train just before you
hit the switch track up ahead.

1379
01:32:53,443 --> 01:32:54,444
Doc!

1380
01:32:54,527 --> 01:32:56,529
Uncouple the cars
from the tender!

1381
01:33:23,348 --> 01:33:25,892
I've wanted to do that
all my life.

1382
01:33:59,342 --> 01:34:00,593
What are these things,
anyway?

1383
01:34:00,677 --> 01:34:02,512
My own version
of Pres-to-Logs.

1384
01:34:02,595 --> 01:34:03,995
Compressed wood
with anthracite dust

1385
01:34:04,055 --> 01:34:06,307
chemically treated to
burn hotter and longer.

1386
01:34:06,391 --> 01:34:08,810
I use them in my forge
so I don't have to stoke it.

1387
01:34:08,893 --> 01:34:10,933
These three in the furnace
will ignite sequentially,

1388
01:34:10,979 --> 01:34:12,397
make the fire
burn hotter,

1389
01:34:12,480 --> 01:34:13,648
kick up
the boiler pressure,

1390
01:34:13,731 --> 01:34:15,108
and make the train
go faster.

1391
01:34:26,995 --> 01:34:28,830
Ready to roll!

1392
01:35:00,486 --> 01:35:01,779
Emmett!

1393
01:35:06,451 --> 01:35:08,912
Marty, are the
time circuits on?

1394
01:35:10,121 --> 01:35:11,122
Check, Doc!

1395
01:35:11,789 --> 01:35:13,875
Input the destination time.

1396
01:35:13,958 --> 01:35:17,670
October 27, 1985,
11:00 a.m.

1397
01:35:19,088 --> 01:35:23,426
Check. Okay, we're cruising at
a steady 25 miles an hour, Doc.

1398
01:35:23,509 --> 01:35:25,303
I'm throwing in
the Pres-to-Logs.

1399
01:35:34,979 --> 01:35:36,773
Marty, the new gauge
on the dashboard

1400
01:35:36,856 --> 01:35:38,441
will tell us
the boiler temperature.

1401
01:35:38,524 --> 01:35:41,903
It's color-coded to indicate
when each log will fire.

1402
01:35:41,986 --> 01:35:43,488
Green, yellow and red.

1403
01:35:44,322 --> 01:35:45,907
Each detonation
will be accompanied

1404
01:35:45,990 --> 01:35:47,825
by a sudden burst
of acceleration.

1405
01:35:49,994 --> 01:35:51,913
Hopefully, we'll hit
88 miles per hour

1406
01:35:51,996 --> 01:35:54,874
before the needle
gets much past 2,000.

1407
01:35:54,958 --> 01:35:57,669
Why? What happens
after it hits 2,000?

1408
01:35:57,752 --> 01:35:59,545
The whole boiler
explodes!

1409
01:36:00,505 --> 01:36:01,589
Perfect!

1410
01:36:17,563 --> 01:36:19,440
Hey, Doc!
We just hit 35!

1411
01:36:19,524 --> 01:36:21,401
Okay, Marty.
I'm coming aboard!

1412
01:36:32,203 --> 01:36:33,204
Emmett!

1413
01:36:34,372 --> 01:36:35,957
Emmett!

1414
01:36:38,126 --> 01:36:39,711
Come on. Come on.

1415
01:36:49,262 --> 01:36:50,680
You better hold
onto something, Doc!

1416
01:36:50,763 --> 01:36:52,265
The yellow log
is about to blow!

1417
01:36:57,520 --> 01:36:58,563
Golly.

1418
01:37:02,233 --> 01:37:03,901
We just passed 40, Doc!

1419
01:37:26,466 --> 01:37:27,633
Emmett!

1420
01:37:29,093 --> 01:37:30,803
Emmett!

1421
01:37:36,642 --> 01:37:38,186
Emmett!

1422
01:37:40,605 --> 01:37:43,274
We just passed 45, Doc!
Go for it!

1423
01:37:53,659 --> 01:37:54,660
Fifty.

1424
01:38:05,797 --> 01:38:06,839
Emmett!

1425
01:38:06,923 --> 01:38:07,924
Clara!

1426
01:38:08,925 --> 01:38:10,343
I love you!

1427
01:38:12,470 --> 01:38:14,764
Doc! Doc!
What's happening?

1428
01:38:14,847 --> 01:38:18,017
It's Clara!
She's on the train!

1429
01:38:18,601 --> 01:38:20,019
Clara? Perfect.

1430
01:38:20,645 --> 01:38:24,232
She's in the cab!
I'm going back for her!

1431
01:38:24,315 --> 01:38:25,400
The windmill!

1432
01:38:25,483 --> 01:38:29,404
Doc! The windmill! We're going
past 50! You'll never make it!

1433
01:38:31,447 --> 01:38:33,282
Then we'll have to
take her back with us!

1434
01:38:33,366 --> 01:38:35,868
Keep calling out
the speed!

1435
01:38:35,952 --> 01:38:39,330
Clara,
climb out here to me!

1436
01:38:39,414 --> 01:38:40,706
I don't know if I can!

1437
01:38:40,790 --> 01:38:42,250
You can do it!

1438
01:38:42,333 --> 01:38:44,460
Just don't look down!

1439
01:38:47,922 --> 01:38:49,173
That's it!

1440
01:39:03,354 --> 01:39:05,022
Sixty miles
an hour, Doc!

1441
01:39:08,151 --> 01:39:11,320
You're doing fine!
Nice and steady!

1442
01:39:14,699 --> 01:39:16,200
Come on!

1443
01:39:16,909 --> 01:39:18,995
Just a little further!

1444
01:39:19,078 --> 01:39:21,038
I can't, Emmett!
I'm scared!

1445
01:39:23,541 --> 01:39:24,542
Seventy!

1446
01:39:25,543 --> 01:39:27,295
Keep coming, Clara!

1447
01:39:28,629 --> 01:39:31,674
Come on! Come on!
You're doing fine!

1448
01:39:32,884 --> 01:39:36,596
Come on!
Nice and easy!

1449
01:39:37,847 --> 01:39:40,683
Don't look down! That's
it! You're doing fine!

1450
01:39:43,603 --> 01:39:47,732
Doc! The red log
is about to blow!

1451
01:39:55,406 --> 01:39:56,407
Clara!

1452
01:39:59,243 --> 01:40:00,912
Emmett!

1453
01:40:10,796 --> 01:40:12,465
I'm trying
to reach you!

1454
01:40:12,548 --> 01:40:13,799
Clara, hold on!

1455
01:40:13,883 --> 01:40:15,092
I can't!

1456
01:40:23,142 --> 01:40:24,185
Oh!

1457
01:40:25,311 --> 01:40:27,146
Doc, I'm gonna slip you
the Hoverboard!

1458
01:40:34,946 --> 01:40:36,447
Marty, watch out!

1459
01:40:44,956 --> 01:40:46,457
Emmett, help!

1460
01:40:49,126 --> 01:40:50,711
Hold on, Clara!

1461
01:40:54,048 --> 01:40:55,633
Doc, ready, Doc?

1462
01:40:56,884 --> 01:40:57,927
Catch it!

1463
01:40:59,679 --> 01:41:00,930
Yes! Yes!

1464
01:41:11,607 --> 01:41:12,858
Emmett!

1465
01:41:12,942 --> 01:41:14,318
Hold on!

1466
01:41:17,989 --> 01:41:18,990
Yes!

1467
01:43:59,483 --> 01:44:00,818
Well, Doc,

1468
01:44:02,528 --> 01:44:04,280
it's destroyed.

1469
01:44:06,490 --> 01:44:08,492
Just like you wanted.

1470
01:44:27,428 --> 01:44:28,512
Hey, butthead,
get away...

1471
01:44:28,596 --> 01:44:29,597
Watch it, Biff!

1472
01:44:29,680 --> 01:44:31,599
Marty, I didn't mean
to scare you.

1473
01:44:31,682 --> 01:44:32,993
I didn't recognize you
in those clothes.

1474
01:44:33,017 --> 01:44:34,435
What the hell
are you doing?

1475
01:44:34,518 --> 01:44:37,271
Just putting on
the second coat now.

1476
01:44:38,397 --> 01:44:39,774
You going cowboy, huh?

1477
01:44:39,857 --> 01:44:42,568
Come on, guys. We're
gonna be late for brunch.

1478
01:44:42,651 --> 01:44:43,652
Coming, Dave.

1479
01:44:43,736 --> 01:44:45,056
Come on, Dad,
we're not gonna hold

1480
01:44:45,112 --> 01:44:46,614
your reservations
all day.

1481
01:44:46,697 --> 01:44:48,115
I can't
find my glasses.

1482
01:44:48,199 --> 01:44:49,551
Lorraine, have you
seen my glasses?

1483
01:44:49,575 --> 01:44:51,619
They're in your
suede jacket, honey.

1484
01:44:51,702 --> 01:44:54,371
Marty, what's wrong? We
thought you went to the lake.

1485
01:44:54,455 --> 01:44:55,623
You wore that
to the lake?

1486
01:44:55,706 --> 01:44:57,625
Thank God you guys
are all back to normal.

1487
01:44:57,708 --> 01:44:59,710
Marty, who are you supposed
to be? Clint Eastwood?

1488
01:45:01,045 --> 01:45:03,589
Right.
I gotta go get Jennifer.

1489
01:45:07,843 --> 01:45:09,386
I really like that hat, Marty.

1490
01:45:09,470 --> 01:45:11,013
Thanks, Biff.

1491
01:45:25,402 --> 01:45:26,737
Jennifer.

1492
01:45:28,781 --> 01:45:30,032
Jen.

1493
01:45:34,286 --> 01:45:35,579
Jennifer.

1494
01:45:58,686 --> 01:45:59,979
Marty.

1495
01:46:01,355 --> 01:46:02,731
Marty.

1496
01:46:04,275 --> 01:46:06,151
I had the worst
nightmare.

1497
01:46:12,616 --> 01:46:16,078
Marty, that dream
I had is so real.

1498
01:46:16,161 --> 01:46:18,622
It was about
the future.

1499
01:46:18,706 --> 01:46:20,708
About us.

1500
01:46:20,791 --> 01:46:23,043
And you got fired.

1501
01:46:23,127 --> 01:46:25,462
Wait a minute. What
do you mean I got fired?

1502
01:46:26,380 --> 01:46:27,590
"Hilldale."

1503
01:46:28,883 --> 01:46:32,219
Hilldale!
This is where we live.

1504
01:46:32,303 --> 01:46:35,306
I mean, this is where
we're going to live.

1505
01:46:36,807 --> 01:46:37,850
Someday.

1506
01:46:41,103 --> 01:46:44,315
Marty, it was a dream,
wasn't it?

1507
01:46:48,193 --> 01:46:49,528
Hey.

1508
01:46:49,612 --> 01:46:51,739
The big M.

1509
01:46:51,822 --> 01:46:53,824
How's it hanging, McFly?

1510
01:46:53,908 --> 01:46:55,159
Hey, Needles.

1511
01:46:55,242 --> 01:46:59,371
Nice set of wheels. Let's
see what she can do.

1512
01:46:59,455 --> 01:47:00,539
Next green light.

1513
01:47:00,623 --> 01:47:01,665
No, thanks.

1514
01:47:03,000 --> 01:47:05,544
What's the matter?
Chicken?

1515
01:47:06,045 --> 01:47:07,588
Chicken McFly.

1516
01:47:07,838 --> 01:47:08,838
Whoo!

1517
01:47:10,215 --> 01:47:11,675
Marty, don't.

1518
01:47:11,759 --> 01:47:13,177
Grab hold of something.

1519
01:47:14,178 --> 01:47:15,888
Come on!
Get on!

1520
01:47:17,306 --> 01:47:19,516
Yeah, go! Go, go, go!

1521
01:47:42,164 --> 01:47:43,499
Did you
do that on purpose?

1522
01:47:43,582 --> 01:47:46,043
Yeah, you think I'm stupid
enough to race that asshole?

1523
01:47:52,257 --> 01:47:55,260
Jeez! I would have
hit that Rolls-Royce.

1524
01:48:05,104 --> 01:48:06,897
It erased.

1525
01:48:20,953 --> 01:48:22,830
You're right.
There's not much left.

1526
01:48:25,416 --> 01:48:27,459
Doc's never
coming back.

1527
01:48:41,932 --> 01:48:43,559
I'm sure gonna
miss him, Jen.

1528
01:48:56,238 --> 01:48:57,322
What the hell?

1529
01:49:14,757 --> 01:49:15,883
Doc!

1530
01:49:16,383 --> 01:49:17,468
Marty!

1531
01:49:17,551 --> 01:49:19,720
Doc! Doc!

1532
01:49:19,803 --> 01:49:22,681
Marty,
it runs on steam!

1533
01:49:27,978 --> 01:49:31,482
Meet the family.
Clara, you know.

1534
01:49:31,565 --> 01:49:32,733
Hi, Marty!

1535
01:49:32,816 --> 01:49:33,901
Ma'am!

1536
01:49:34,943 --> 01:49:37,112
These are our boys.

1537
01:49:37,905 --> 01:49:41,700
Jules and Verne.

1538
01:49:44,828 --> 01:49:47,664
Boys, this is
Marty and Jennifer.

1539
01:49:50,667 --> 01:49:52,628
Doc, I thought I'd
never see you again.

1540
01:49:52,711 --> 01:49:54,922
You can't keep
a good scientist down.

1541
01:49:55,005 --> 01:49:57,132
After all, I had to
come back for Einstein,

1542
01:49:57,216 --> 01:50:00,511
and, well, I didn't want
you to be worried about me.

1543
01:50:01,136 --> 01:50:02,137
Oh!

1544
01:50:02,596 --> 01:50:05,057
I brought you
a little souvenir.

1545
01:50:21,740 --> 01:50:24,910
It's great, Doc.
Thanks.

1546
01:50:27,287 --> 01:50:31,125
Dr. Brown, I brought this
note back from the future,

1547
01:50:31,208 --> 01:50:33,460
and now it's erased.

1548
01:50:33,544 --> 01:50:35,504
Of course
it's erased.

1549
01:50:37,422 --> 01:50:38,757
But what does
that mean?

1550
01:50:38,841 --> 01:50:42,719
It means your future
hasn't been written yet.

1551
01:50:42,803 --> 01:50:44,555
No one's has.

1552
01:50:44,638 --> 01:50:47,057
Your future is
whatever you make it.

1553
01:50:47,141 --> 01:50:49,685
So make it a good one,
both of you.

1554
01:50:52,813 --> 01:50:54,022
We will, Doc!

1555
01:50:54,106 --> 01:50:55,649
Stand back!

1556
01:50:56,150 --> 01:50:58,235
All right, boys,
buckle up!

1557
01:51:03,240 --> 01:51:08,412
Hey, Doc, where are you
going now? Back to the future?

1558
01:51:08,996 --> 01:51:12,249
No, already been there.

